WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:08.475
So, this afternoon we
have a great treat.

00:00:08.475 --> 00:00:11.545
We're going to be joined
by President Salovey.

00:00:11.545 --> 00:00:15.515
This is our third year of the
Day of Data and each year we

00:00:15.515 --> 00:00:18.852
strive to get a senior
administrator to come and talk

00:00:18.852 --> 00:00:20.721
to us and this year
we struck it big.

00:00:20.721 --> 00:00:22.556
We got the university President.

00:00:22.556 --> 00:00:28.328
He has pushed aside the provost
this year, but Ben was okay

00:00:28.328 --> 00:00:32.366
with it, and then we'll be
hearing from President Salovey.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.737
He has a long career here
at Yale where he started as a

00:00:36.737 --> 00:00:40.774
graduate student and was a
professor of psychology, and

00:00:40.774 --> 00:00:43.477
through all of that work,
himself has worked with data

00:00:43.477 --> 00:00:47.481
a lot, so this is both important
to Yale as a whole, but also to

00:00:47.481 --> 00:00:48.815
his own personal research.

00:00:48.815 --> 00:00:51.652
So, let me introduce
President Salovey.

00:00:52.386 --> 00:00:57.090
(Applause)

00:00:58.892 --> 00:01:02.095
Well, thank you very much,
Susan, and welcome everybody.

00:01:02.095 --> 00:01:06.033
You know, I just want to
say something about Susan.

00:01:06.033 --> 00:01:11.471
When we hired a university
librarian a few years back we

00:01:11.471 --> 00:01:16.343
were looking for someone who
would interpret what it means to

00:01:16.343 --> 00:01:22.382
be the university librarian
in the broadest possible way.

00:01:22.382 --> 00:01:26.853
You know, not just a custodian
of book collections, but a kind

00:01:26.853 --> 00:01:31.358
of recognition that the
contemporary university

00:01:31.358 --> 00:01:38.465
librarian is someone who really
facilitates research scholarship

00:01:38.465 --> 00:01:43.804
in teaching by essentially
making resources of all kinds

00:01:43.804 --> 00:01:49.009
that can be archive collected,
organized, interpreted available

00:01:49.009 --> 00:01:53.246
to them, and Susan has
interpreted that charge, that

00:01:53.246 --> 00:01:57.918
mission, that job description as
broadly as anyone on this campus

00:01:57.918 --> 00:02:03.323
and we are very, very fortunate
to have her in that role.

00:02:05.025 --> 00:02:08.161
It's wonderful to have someone
who respects the old and the

00:02:08.161 --> 00:02:14.067
traditional and loves books, but
also is not afraid to embrace

00:02:14.067 --> 00:02:20.007
and rush toward the new, the
entire technological end of all

00:02:20.007 --> 00:02:23.610
of this and she does
both very, very well.

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:26.546
If you really haven't ever
gotten to know her, you should.

00:02:26.546 --> 00:02:30.317
Take a minute and introduce
yourselves and find out what's

00:02:30.317 --> 00:02:32.719
going on in our library system.

00:02:32.719 --> 00:02:35.455
So, it's a pleasure to be here.

00:02:35.455 --> 00:02:39.893
This is the third year of an
outstanding program that has

00:02:39.893 --> 00:02:41.828
proven quite popular
on this campus.

00:02:41.828 --> 00:02:48.135
I think it is a testimonial to
both what libraries do and what

00:02:48.135 --> 00:02:54.341
librarians do, but it also
helps to galvanize a kind of

00:02:54.341 --> 00:03:01.748
collective interest in the use
of data in research and data

00:03:01.748 --> 00:03:07.087
management as well
throughout the campus.

00:03:07.087 --> 00:03:10.991
What I love about the theme
is it's so cross cutting and so

00:03:10.991 --> 00:03:14.728
interdisciplinary, so, as you
heard, I'm a psychologist,

00:03:14.728 --> 00:03:16.963
I work in a data
intensive field.

00:03:16.963 --> 00:03:20.500
Most of our data are either
gathered in experiments or in

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:26.139
big field studies or sometimes
in survey research and sometimes

00:03:26.139 --> 00:03:31.011
use of archival sources, mostly
quantitative and that's only

00:03:31.011 --> 00:03:36.583
just a little tip of the iceberg
of what is possible, but very

00:03:36.583 --> 00:03:41.254
dependent, as a psychologist,
both on what capacities the

00:03:41.254 --> 00:03:45.392
university had to manage data,
make it available, but also

00:03:45.392 --> 00:03:47.594
data analysis interpretation.

00:03:47.594 --> 00:03:52.165
And what I love about this
theme, The Day of Data theme,

00:03:52.165 --> 00:03:55.969
is it really cuts across
disciplines like my own and sort

00:03:55.969 --> 00:04:01.942
of says what are common
ways of managing, planning,

00:04:01.942 --> 00:04:07.481
using, interpreting data.

00:04:07.481 --> 00:04:09.883
I think many of you know, when I
became president two years ago,

00:04:09.883 --> 00:04:13.320
I talked a lot about a more
unified Yale and, in fact,

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:18.091
the theme of the inauguration,
which was two years ago

00:04:18.091 --> 00:04:21.394
next month, was going
to be One Yale.

00:04:21.394 --> 00:04:27.467
And a couple of weeks before we
were going to announce the theme

00:04:27.467 --> 00:04:31.605
One Yale, Harvard announced
their fundraising campaign...

00:04:31.605 --> 00:04:33.106
One Harvard.

00:04:33.106 --> 00:04:33.874
[Laughter]

00:04:33.874 --> 00:04:37.511
And so, we realized that we
had to do One Yale implicitly,

00:04:37.511 --> 00:04:40.413
and so, you may have remembered
posters and tote bags and

00:04:40.413 --> 00:04:43.049
things that would say Many
Disciplines, Many People,

00:04:43.049 --> 00:04:46.520
Many Nationalities, Many
Departments, many this,

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:49.089
many that, many this,
many that... Yale.

00:04:49.089 --> 00:04:49.623
Right?

00:04:49.623 --> 00:04:52.025
It was one Yale - it was
the implicit One Yale.

00:04:52.025 --> 00:04:57.397
Well, you know, when it comes to
thinking about data, numerical,

00:04:57.397 --> 00:05:02.402
geographical, textual, visual,
I think we are one Yale.

00:05:02.402 --> 00:05:09.943
I think we can take a kind of
unified approach to management,

00:05:09.943 --> 00:05:15.081
organization, accessibility,
consultation... all the tools

00:05:15.081 --> 00:05:19.486
and assistance that anyone in
this room and beyond might need

00:05:19.486 --> 00:05:25.158
and, as a result, do it in a
way that makes this among the

00:05:25.158 --> 00:05:28.995
easier places to
actually get work done.

00:05:32.432 --> 00:05:35.335
You know, you just have to
look around a little bit at

00:05:35.335 --> 00:05:41.741
university initiatives of the
last year to see the ways in

00:05:41.741 --> 00:05:49.115
which research data can be used
to address complicated needs of

00:05:49.115 --> 00:05:53.053
this place, so, you know, the
opening... I make it sound like

00:05:53.053 --> 00:05:55.055
it was yesterday, it wasn't
yesterday... but the opening of

00:05:55.055 --> 00:05:58.358
the Center for Science and
Social Science Information was

00:05:58.358 --> 00:06:03.296
created three years ago
explicitly to support data

00:06:03.296 --> 00:06:07.734
management and analysis in
what had originally been a

00:06:07.734 --> 00:06:11.605
wonderful home for
largely biology books.

00:06:11.605 --> 00:06:15.875
The Yale Center for Research and
Computing which is supporting

00:06:15.875 --> 00:06:20.480
advanced computing, data
processing technology makes its

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:23.016
resources available throughout
the research community.

00:06:23.016 --> 00:06:26.419
The university library's
Interdisciplinary Digital

00:06:26.419 --> 00:06:30.423
Humanities Lab provides
expertise, equipment, facilities

00:06:30.423 --> 00:06:34.628
to faculty and students often
in fields who didn't think of

00:06:34.628 --> 00:06:38.932
themselves as data intensive
fields but now discover what

00:06:38.932 --> 00:06:41.568
they can do with data.

00:06:42.369 --> 00:06:46.039
There's a data policy committee
appointed by the provost and

00:06:46.039 --> 00:06:48.575
charged with developing
guidelines for acquisition,

00:06:48.575 --> 00:06:52.112
management, storage and
they're going to release their

00:06:52.112 --> 00:06:54.514
recommendations
later this semester.

00:06:54.514 --> 00:06:59.552
So... I'm hoping that the
Day of Data - my guess is the

00:06:59.552 --> 00:07:01.855
Day of Data has something
in it for everyone.

00:07:01.855 --> 00:07:06.026
I hope it stimulates you
to think about new ideas,

00:07:06.026 --> 00:07:10.664
new approaches to information,
new opportunities, maybe new

00:07:10.664 --> 00:07:15.669
supports and services
that Yale provides that you

00:07:15.669 --> 00:07:16.536
didn't know about.

00:07:16.536 --> 00:07:20.440
If that happens, that would be
a great thing because we want

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:26.313
nothing more than the ability
for you to produce world class

00:07:26.313 --> 00:07:30.784
scholarship and research and
then to teach, to provide the

00:07:30.784 --> 00:07:33.887
best teaching, I always say the
most excellent teaching but that

00:07:33.887 --> 00:07:36.289
sounds like - what are
those characters who have

00:07:36.289 --> 00:07:38.091
the excellent adventure?

00:07:40.126 --> 00:07:40.860
Bill and Ted?

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:41.428
Bill and Ted.

00:07:41.428 --> 00:07:43.963
It sounds like Bill and Ted
when I say and provide the most

00:07:43.963 --> 00:07:47.400
excellent education in a
research university environment.

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:49.969
This is part of how we do that.

00:07:49.969 --> 00:07:52.605
I don't want to take more time
but because it does give me

00:07:52.605 --> 00:07:56.743
great pleasure to introduce
our keynote speaker today.

00:07:57.577 --> 00:07:59.946
This is Chaitan Baru.

00:07:59.946 --> 00:08:03.016
He's from the University
of California, San Diego.

00:08:03.016 --> 00:08:05.985
He currently serves as the
National Science Foundation

00:08:05.985 --> 00:08:09.789
Senior Advisor for Data Science.

00:08:09.789 --> 00:08:14.127
In that role at NSF, he supports
collaborative efforts pertaining

00:08:14.127 --> 00:08:18.832
to big data and he's going
to speak today on the topic of

00:08:18.832 --> 00:08:22.202
Data Science R&amp;D:
Current Activities

00:08:22.202 --> 00:08:23.803
and Future Directions.

00:08:23.803 --> 00:08:28.341
So, let me ask you to join me
in welcoming Chaitan Baru with

00:08:28.341 --> 00:08:30.443
a very warm round of applause.

00:08:30.443 --> 00:08:31.177
Thanks.

00:08:31.177 --> 00:08:36.716
(Applause)

00:08:40.687 --> 00:08:43.857
Just a second as
I get this fired up.

00:09:04.344 --> 00:09:08.348
Well, thanks very much for
that introduction and actually,

00:09:08.348 --> 00:09:12.185
thanks to the organizers
for inviting me here to

00:09:12.185 --> 00:09:12.852
give this talk.

00:09:12.852 --> 00:09:14.521
It's a real pleasure.

00:09:14.521 --> 00:09:18.591
What I've heard so far in
the session, in the morning,

00:09:18.591 --> 00:09:21.594
I think there's a lot of
excitement across campus in the

00:09:21.594 --> 00:09:22.796
area of data.

00:09:22.796 --> 00:09:24.697
This is your third
year to do this.

00:09:24.697 --> 00:09:28.368
My sense is each year
this has been building up.

00:09:28.368 --> 00:09:32.071
It must be because there's
so much going on right now that

00:09:32.071 --> 00:09:33.540
I can see.

00:09:34.407 --> 00:09:38.978
So, this is my first visit
to Yale in this capacity.

00:09:38.978 --> 00:09:42.382
I think I've been here once
maybe 15 years ago as a tourist,

00:09:42.382 --> 00:09:44.317
just touring New England,
happened to go

00:09:44.317 --> 00:09:45.452
through New Haven.

00:09:45.452 --> 00:09:47.720
But I took the train up
from Washington, D.C.

00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:50.123
...it's actually a
very nice train ride.

00:09:50.123 --> 00:09:52.592
You see some scenic
spots along the way.

00:09:52.592 --> 00:09:54.527
So, very happy to be here.

00:09:54.527 --> 00:09:59.332
I think my task in this next
hour using the clock that

00:09:59.332 --> 00:10:05.705
actually works as Bob found out,
is A) not to put you to sleep

00:10:05.705 --> 00:10:10.376
right after a nice Panera
sandwich; and B) to just give

00:10:10.376 --> 00:10:17.383
you sort of, let's say, 25,000
to 30,000-foot level view of the

00:10:17.383 --> 00:10:21.187
variety of data science, big
data activities that are going

00:10:21.187 --> 00:10:24.791
on, mostly at NSF and I
love to say a little bit of

00:10:24.791 --> 00:10:26.726
the interagency things.

00:10:26.726 --> 00:10:29.362
So, hopefully, you'll get a
sense of the landscape of things

00:10:29.362 --> 00:10:31.598
that are happening.

00:10:31.598 --> 00:10:37.837
Needless to say, this is a
really exciting times for data.

00:10:37.837 --> 00:10:41.207
We'll get into some of these
during my presentation.

00:10:41.207 --> 00:10:43.810
As everyone says, there's
nothing new about data,

00:10:43.810 --> 00:10:46.980
especially scientists will say
that's what we've been doing all

00:10:46.980 --> 00:10:50.817
along, so we'll touch a little
bit upon what makes it new

00:10:50.817 --> 00:10:53.720
and different now.

00:10:53.720 --> 00:10:57.156
The reason I actually decided
to leave San Diego and

00:10:57.156 --> 00:10:59.893
go to Washington, D.C. and
take this - this is actually a

00:10:59.893 --> 00:11:02.762
new position at NSF,
Senior Advisor for Data Science,

00:11:02.762 --> 00:11:06.132
and I thought this is a really
interesting time to be there,

00:11:06.132 --> 00:11:10.003
both to help NSF think
through some of these plans

00:11:10.003 --> 00:11:11.971
and do some of those
interagency things.

00:11:11.971 --> 00:11:13.139
So...

00:11:13.673 --> 00:11:16.176
All right, so,
let's get started.

00:11:16.176 --> 00:11:22.749
So, I purposely used data
science R&amp;D in my title.

00:11:22.749 --> 00:11:27.453
It doesn't say big data
and I'll come to that.

00:11:27.453 --> 00:11:31.190
The presentation will primarily
be, of course, from the point of

00:11:31.190 --> 00:11:32.559
view of NSF.

00:11:32.559 --> 00:11:36.863
So, NSF's perspective and role
in the federal government is,

00:11:36.863 --> 00:11:40.233
you know, it's the agency that
supports basic curiosity doing

00:11:40.233 --> 00:11:44.137
research and these are the words
from the actual mission of NSF:

00:11:44.137 --> 00:11:47.307
to promote the progress of
science; to advance the national

00:11:47.307 --> 00:11:51.945
health, prosperity, and welfare;
to secure the national defense.

00:11:51.945 --> 00:11:56.316
Actually, after I went to NSF I
went over Bush's original report

00:11:56.316 --> 00:11:59.819
that put NSF together and, in
fact, what is now filed in that

00:11:59.819 --> 00:12:05.224
all was in one organization
and then things split later on.

00:12:05.224 --> 00:12:07.627
So, let's get into
the topic right away.

00:12:07.627 --> 00:12:11.631
This whole phenomena of big
data, which I don't have to

00:12:11.631 --> 00:12:15.368
define, you already heard that
from Bob this morning, I think

00:12:15.368 --> 00:12:19.439
fundamentally what it has done,
why are things different now

00:12:19.439 --> 00:12:23.243
considering that scientists have
always dealt with data is just

00:12:23.243 --> 00:12:27.647
the amount of data and our
ability to so easily collect so

00:12:27.647 --> 00:12:31.017
many different types of data for
any problem that we are looking

00:12:31.017 --> 00:12:37.257
at has essentially forced on
us greater awareness of data.

00:12:37.257 --> 00:12:43.162
It's no longer taking some data
set that you collect or somebody

00:12:43.162 --> 00:12:45.498
else, in fact, primarily
that you collected and did

00:12:45.498 --> 00:12:46.466
something with it.

00:12:46.466 --> 00:12:49.369
Increasingly there are data sets
that others have collected,

00:12:49.369 --> 00:12:51.270
increasingly there are
requirements, some of which

00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:52.639
might come from the
federal government.

00:12:52.639 --> 00:12:56.242
Let's say you have to make this
data available for a long term,

00:12:56.242 --> 00:12:58.711
and so, you start worrying
about what does that mean.

00:12:58.711 --> 00:13:02.281
Meta data, context,
sustainability, etcetera.

00:13:02.281 --> 00:13:05.184
So, the way I would say this is
there's just greater awareness

00:13:05.184 --> 00:13:07.320
of all aspects of data.

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:11.691
And this goes, by the way, not
just in science but in business.

00:13:11.691 --> 00:13:16.529
As you know, the term really
sort of got adopted by folks in

00:13:16.529 --> 00:13:21.567
business and Strata Hadoop
conferences later this month...

00:13:21.567 --> 00:13:22.802
I think Bob mentioned it.

00:13:22.802 --> 00:13:24.237
I'm actually planning to attend.

00:13:24.237 --> 00:13:25.638
I hope some of
you will be there.

00:13:25.638 --> 00:13:29.876
It's really a totally energetic
and exciting place to be.

00:13:29.876 --> 00:13:32.512
If you just want to see the
extent of stuff that's happening

00:13:32.512 --> 00:13:35.315
in industry, Strata Hadoop
conference is a good

00:13:35.315 --> 00:13:36.349
place to be.

00:13:36.349 --> 00:13:40.687
So, while there is in industry
or in science, we've sort of

00:13:40.687 --> 00:13:44.123
thought about traditionally
it's been about just looking at

00:13:44.123 --> 00:13:46.759
cleaned up, structured data
and doing the analytics.

00:13:46.759 --> 00:13:52.665
Now, we realize that, as we get
not just designed data, but what

00:13:52.665 --> 00:13:55.968
is called found data, so not
just data that you created but

00:13:55.968 --> 00:13:59.305
maybe that others have put
together and you're using it in

00:13:59.305 --> 00:14:03.609
some way in your analysis, you
realize that you worry about the

00:14:03.609 --> 00:14:05.578
entire life cycle of data.

00:14:05.578 --> 00:14:08.948
You have to have much more
awareness of what is the context

00:14:08.948 --> 00:14:11.350
in which this data was
collected, what is the context

00:14:11.350 --> 00:14:12.885
in which I'm reusing it.

00:14:12.885 --> 00:14:15.021
It's all types of data,
it's unstructured,

00:14:15.021 --> 00:14:15.822
some is structured.

00:14:15.822 --> 00:14:18.991
We heard about text
analysis this morning.

00:14:18.991 --> 00:14:21.060
There's all sorts of image
data that we collect.

00:14:21.060 --> 00:14:24.931
There's maps... spatial,
temporal... all of that, and as

00:14:24.931 --> 00:14:28.167
context becomes important and
reuse becomes important it's as

00:14:28.167 --> 00:14:31.370
much about the meta data so that
there is some understanding of

00:14:31.370 --> 00:14:35.074
what this original data set was
as it is about the data itself.

00:14:35.074 --> 00:14:37.610
And at the end of the day,
all of this has to be processed

00:14:37.610 --> 00:14:38.644
efficiently and used.

00:14:38.644 --> 00:14:42.448
So, if you collect data an
you never use it, it's like not

00:14:42.448 --> 00:14:44.050
collecting data at all, right?

00:14:44.050 --> 00:14:48.654
And also, if you look at in
business, and increasingly in

00:14:48.654 --> 00:14:51.457
some of the sciences that are
collecting lots of data...

00:14:51.457 --> 00:14:55.461
actually astronomy comes to
mind, but increasingly I would

00:14:55.461 --> 00:14:59.165
think things like genomics,
etcetera, there is this issue

00:14:59.165 --> 00:15:00.032
of timely use.

00:15:00.032 --> 00:15:04.470
I think we are pushing more and
more and more towards real time

00:15:04.470 --> 00:15:06.205
online analysis of data.

00:15:06.205 --> 00:15:08.875
If you think of it, that's
actually one way by which you

00:15:08.875 --> 00:15:11.911
can avoid collecting a whole lot
of data if you can just process

00:15:11.911 --> 00:15:15.948
it in time and make use of it or
not and check it or maybe just

00:15:15.948 --> 00:15:17.650
keep a representation of it.

00:15:17.650 --> 00:15:24.123
So, essentially, as was
mentioned, I'm originally from

00:15:24.123 --> 00:15:28.027
the University of California in
San Diego at the Super Computer

00:15:28.027 --> 00:15:31.164
Center, but my background
is as a computer scientist.

00:15:31.164 --> 00:15:33.432
I'm in the field
called database systems.

00:15:33.432 --> 00:15:36.936
So, if you are in the data
field, these are absolutely

00:15:36.936 --> 00:15:39.906
exciting times because all the
stuff that you did 10-15 years

00:15:39.906 --> 00:15:43.309
ago is all coming back, and
in even more important ways.

00:15:43.309 --> 00:15:46.846
So, that's definitely
rejuvenated and created a new

00:15:46.846 --> 00:15:49.148
research agenda around data.

00:15:49.148 --> 00:15:50.716
Actually, there was a
question I wanted to ask,

00:15:50.716 --> 00:15:51.617
so let me do it now.

00:15:51.617 --> 00:15:55.154
It's one of those strategies
to make sure that you're

00:15:55.154 --> 00:15:58.257
not totally asleep.

00:15:58.257 --> 00:16:01.260
So, let's see, how many
folks in the audience

00:16:01.260 --> 00:16:04.297
are faculty members?

00:16:04.297 --> 00:16:04.664
Okay.

00:16:04.664 --> 00:16:05.097
That's good.

00:16:05.097 --> 00:16:08.334
There are some, but there's lots
of non faculty, so I assume

00:16:08.334 --> 00:16:10.870
a lot of them are grad students,
maybe some are staff.

00:16:10.870 --> 00:16:13.906
How many in the audience
are computer scientists?

00:16:15.308 --> 00:16:15.641
Right.

00:16:15.641 --> 00:16:17.677
So, I think there needs to
be more computer scientists

00:16:17.677 --> 00:16:24.383
in the room, but that's
one of the things I will

00:16:24.383 --> 00:16:25.885
talk about, data science.

00:16:25.885 --> 00:16:26.819
In fact, here it is.

00:16:26.819 --> 00:16:31.123
So, if you look at NSF, the
kinds of things that NSF funds,

00:16:31.123 --> 00:16:38.130
it stretches the whole range
from basic research on one side

00:16:38.130 --> 00:16:40.433
and infrastructure
on the other side.

00:16:40.433 --> 00:16:43.769
Now, the infrastructure that NSF
funds, of course, is in support

00:16:43.769 --> 00:16:47.406
of basic research, so usually
it's an instrument of some type,

00:16:47.406 --> 00:16:50.543
you know, regardless of
which directory that you're in,

00:16:50.543 --> 00:16:54.914
whether it's geosciences or
astronomy or ecology, etcetera,

00:16:54.914 --> 00:16:57.416
these are all about building
state of the art instruments

00:16:57.416 --> 00:16:59.952
that can collect more data
and, of course, that's what

00:16:59.952 --> 00:17:02.054
they're all doing.

00:17:02.054 --> 00:17:05.892
NSF has programs across the
spectrum, basically search,

00:17:05.892 --> 00:17:11.163
as well as applied areas, there
are programs for, for example,

00:17:11.163 --> 00:17:14.867
creating software that could be
used by a discipline and maybe

00:17:14.867 --> 00:17:15.868
re-used by others.

00:17:15.868 --> 00:17:17.336
There are programs
for networking.

00:17:17.336 --> 00:17:21.140
There are programs for actually
having super computers and

00:17:21.140 --> 00:17:24.210
storage and there are
big programs in many

00:17:24.210 --> 00:17:26.245
of the directorates.

00:17:26.245 --> 00:17:30.216
I don't think computer science
particularly doesn't have any,

00:17:30.216 --> 00:17:32.184
but many of the other
directorates have programs

00:17:32.184 --> 00:17:33.185
for data archives.

00:17:33.185 --> 00:17:36.522
These are community scientific
data archives, ICPS already was

00:17:36.522 --> 00:17:38.591
mentioned, and there
are many others.

00:17:38.591 --> 00:17:44.263
And there is, of course, a
division at NSF called the ACI,

00:17:44.263 --> 00:17:47.767
which is part of CISE that funds
things on the infrastructure

00:17:47.767 --> 00:17:52.371
side and education and
training is also a big part

00:17:52.371 --> 00:17:53.506
of all of this, right?

00:17:53.506 --> 00:17:56.142
So, there's education and
training, both on the basic side

00:17:56.142 --> 00:18:00.012
and the infrastructure side,
that is, there are programs all

00:18:00.012 --> 00:18:04.216
the way from doing research in
education to supporting graduate

00:18:04.216 --> 00:18:07.720
education, the NRT program
that's there, which used to be

00:18:07.720 --> 00:18:10.923
the IGERT, but there
are also programs more on

00:18:10.923 --> 00:18:11.991
the training side.

00:18:11.991 --> 00:18:14.827
So, if you are a scientist, you
want to use some computational

00:18:14.827 --> 00:18:18.297
resources, increasingly I hope
there are also such things

00:18:18.297 --> 00:18:19.365
for data resources.

00:18:19.365 --> 00:18:24.637
Then there are programs that
allow for training in that area.

00:18:24.637 --> 00:18:29.342
So, this whole field of big data
and now introduced data science

00:18:29.342 --> 00:18:31.010
sits across all of that.

00:18:31.010 --> 00:18:35.982
There are activities at NSF that
are in that box, or that circle,

00:18:35.982 --> 00:18:39.518
that cut across from basic
research to infrastructure to

00:18:39.518 --> 00:18:40.753
education and training.

00:18:40.753 --> 00:18:42.455
I'll say a little
bit about that.

00:18:42.455 --> 00:18:48.928
What has this done then, data
science, and actually it's in

00:18:48.928 --> 00:18:51.430
the title of the position I
have: I'm the senior advisor

00:18:51.430 --> 00:18:52.999
for data science.

00:18:52.999 --> 00:18:55.501
And one way I like to think
about it, and maybe it's partly

00:18:55.501 --> 00:18:59.638
because I'm also from a super
computer center, is, you know,

00:18:59.638 --> 00:19:03.209
big data is to data science
as supercomputing was

00:19:03.209 --> 00:19:04.410
to computational science.

00:19:04.410 --> 00:19:07.847
In other words, I think there
was a reference earlier that

00:19:07.847 --> 00:19:09.815
more is something different.

00:19:09.815 --> 00:19:10.116
Right?

00:19:10.116 --> 00:19:12.852
So, when you have more of
something, significantly more of

00:19:12.852 --> 00:19:16.455
something, you really enter a
new state and that's basically

00:19:16.455 --> 00:19:17.957
what supercomputing did.

00:19:17.957 --> 00:19:21.994
When you had that scale
of computing available you

00:19:21.994 --> 00:19:24.263
had all sorts of new
computational methods in all

00:19:24.263 --> 00:19:25.231
sorts of disciplines.

00:19:25.231 --> 00:19:28.834
It's not just physics and
astronomy, it's everything all

00:19:28.834 --> 00:19:32.738
the way down to social sciences
now, and certainly ecology and

00:19:32.738 --> 00:19:37.376
all of those areas looking at
computational techniques to the

00:19:37.376 --> 00:19:41.514
extent that we said, okay,
that's a discipline in which

00:19:41.514 --> 00:19:45.918
some training needs to be given
and there are several, at least

00:19:45.918 --> 00:19:48.754
graduate student programs, in
computational science that get

00:19:48.754 --> 00:19:52.525
you trained in how to
model things that are using

00:19:52.525 --> 00:19:55.728
computational science and
then how to do them at scale.

00:19:55.728 --> 00:19:58.798
This term wasn't mentioned
today, but you may have heard

00:19:58.798 --> 00:20:01.000
of this notion of the
Fourth Paradigm, right?

00:20:01.000 --> 00:20:03.836
So, the third paradigm is
computing, the fourth paradigm

00:20:03.836 --> 00:20:08.040
is data, so computational
science was the third paradigm.

00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:11.877
I think what big data has done,
that is, the availability of so

00:20:11.877 --> 00:20:15.014
much data of so many different
kinds that could be used in so

00:20:15.014 --> 00:20:18.217
many different contexts has
created the need, as I said

00:20:18.217 --> 00:20:21.687
earlier, to understand the full
life cycle of data, to get a

00:20:21.687 --> 00:20:25.958
much more in depth training on
all of those aspects of data and

00:20:25.958 --> 00:20:28.094
not just sort of stay
on the edges of it.

00:20:28.094 --> 00:20:34.066
And I think that basically leads
to creation of data science as

00:20:34.066 --> 00:20:35.167
an endeavor in itself.

00:20:35.167 --> 00:20:41.607
So, I'll say maybe quite a
bit more later on about various

00:20:41.607 --> 00:20:43.976
activities that we are
supporting and the community is

00:20:43.976 --> 00:20:48.547
doing in pushing forward the
data science agenda but actually

00:20:48.547 --> 00:20:51.917
a lot of what I heard this
morning, the Yale Institute for

00:20:51.917 --> 00:20:53.853
Network Science,
is an interesting one.

00:20:53.853 --> 00:20:57.590
There are so many common
problems with data that are seen

00:20:57.590 --> 00:21:02.094
across so many disciplines that,
that itself becomes something

00:21:02.094 --> 00:21:03.295
to be studied.

00:21:03.295 --> 00:21:06.465
And we also heard that even in
something like the law school,

00:21:06.465 --> 00:21:08.501
they would like their students
to receive at least some

00:21:08.501 --> 00:21:12.471
training in data, so there's
also this notion that if you had

00:21:12.471 --> 00:21:15.641
a core body of knowledge,
that information could be

00:21:15.641 --> 00:21:20.146
imparted through specialized
courses to different other

00:21:20.146 --> 00:21:21.280
departments and domains.

00:21:21.280 --> 00:21:25.017
So, I think what is emerging
here is this notion of data

00:21:25.017 --> 00:21:29.588
science and that's why I titled
my talk as Data Science R&amp;D.

00:21:29.588 --> 00:21:34.093
If you zoom into computer
science, as director at NSF,

00:21:34.093 --> 00:21:38.831
we certainly found things on the
foundational research side and

00:21:38.831 --> 00:21:42.134
the other directorates at
NSF, we, computer scientists,

00:21:42.134 --> 00:21:43.369
think of them as the domains.

00:21:43.369 --> 00:21:47.006
Those are the guys who have some
problems that maybe we can help

00:21:47.006 --> 00:21:48.440
them solve it.

00:21:49.041 --> 00:21:54.880
The Big Data program at CISE,
in CISE at NSF, is really the

00:21:54.880 --> 00:21:56.182
one that connects the two.

00:21:56.182 --> 00:22:01.420
So, big data, if you think in
terms of proposals, a successful

00:22:01.420 --> 00:22:05.191
big data proposal is one
that really bridges these two

00:22:05.191 --> 00:22:06.792
in an effective way.

00:22:06.792 --> 00:22:09.228
It's looking at new
fundamental techniques.

00:22:09.228 --> 00:22:14.099
Maybe it's scaling, maybe it's
a new algorithm, with a real

00:22:14.099 --> 00:22:17.469
problem that needs to be
solved in the domain science

00:22:17.469 --> 00:22:19.738
or engineering discipline.

00:22:22.041 --> 00:22:26.445
So, one of the things I do in my
other life, so some of you may

00:22:26.445 --> 00:22:30.082
have been at NSF, but if
you're at NSF there is this

00:22:30.082 --> 00:22:30.916
notion of IRD.

00:22:30.916 --> 00:22:32.651
You can do your independent R&amp;D.

00:22:32.651 --> 00:22:36.655
You get some time to do that and
one of the things I do during my

00:22:36.655 --> 00:22:40.259
independent R&amp;D activities
back in San Diego is related to

00:22:40.259 --> 00:22:44.230
benchmarking of big data
applications and we run a

00:22:44.230 --> 00:22:48.400
workshop every year and the next
one is in December in India.

00:22:48.400 --> 00:22:52.104
And one of the speakers,
once who is from Facebook,

00:22:52.104 --> 00:22:54.707
came and said, big data
to us means big.

00:22:54.707 --> 00:22:57.576
Right, so if you're on Facebook,
the notion of big data,

00:22:57.576 --> 00:22:58.911
it's just big stuff.

00:22:58.911 --> 00:23:00.679
There's petabytes of data.

00:23:00.679 --> 00:23:04.583
Facebook has something like
20,000-30,000 instances of MYSQL

00:23:04.583 --> 00:23:09.355
database because every data
science with Facebook basically

00:23:09.355 --> 00:23:13.025
goes to work every day, pulls
out some data from the big well

00:23:13.025 --> 00:23:15.561
of petabytes of data, creates
their own little database and

00:23:15.561 --> 00:23:19.531
they're trying the next
ad placement algorithm or

00:23:19.531 --> 00:23:21.367
whatever it is, right.

00:23:21.367 --> 00:23:25.738
So, there are some people for
whom big data just means big.

00:23:25.738 --> 00:23:29.808
But what I like to say is
big data is also about data.

00:23:29.808 --> 00:23:31.777
And the data comes from
disciplines, so if you're a

00:23:31.777 --> 00:23:37.416
computer scientist looking
at this, there are increasingly

00:23:37.416 --> 00:23:41.086
areas in computer science
that are sort of data

00:23:41.086 --> 00:23:43.622
intensive... network
engineering is one.

00:23:43.622 --> 00:23:46.191
There are groups that have
collected lots of Internet

00:23:46.191 --> 00:23:50.062
traffic data, but by and large,
at least so far, computer

00:23:50.062 --> 00:23:52.998
science has not been a data
intensive field but what

00:23:52.998 --> 00:23:55.734
computer science has done is
work with data that may come

00:23:55.734 --> 00:23:59.571
from other disciplines, and
I'm talking in terms of a field

00:23:59.571 --> 00:24:03.108
doing its own research.

00:24:03.108 --> 00:24:06.078
So, data has to come from
domains and, therefore,

00:24:06.078 --> 00:24:09.548
if you're doing big data
research as a computer

00:24:09.548 --> 00:24:13.452
scientist, you need to be very
intimately tied to the domains.

00:24:14.820 --> 00:24:18.490
There are certainly examples
of big science data.

00:24:18.490 --> 00:24:22.394
I won't go into all the details
of this, but I'll just give you

00:24:22.394 --> 00:24:27.232
a laundry list of names,
many of these, many of you

00:24:27.232 --> 00:24:29.168
would have heard of.

00:24:29.168 --> 00:24:34.473
LIGO is a physics observatory
generating a petabyte of data

00:24:34.473 --> 00:24:38.777
per year; LHC, Large Hadron
Collider, you've all heard about

00:24:38.777 --> 00:24:45.284
it, it's collecting 4 petabytes
per year; LSST is the newest NSF

00:24:45.284 --> 00:24:49.121
big instrument project that just
got inaugurated down in Chile,

00:24:49.121 --> 00:24:54.927
I mean, the construction has
just started, and that's going

00:24:54.927 --> 00:25:00.899
to generate 100 petabytes in
10 years, at the end of the day

00:25:00.899 --> 00:25:04.570
it'll have a 10 petabyte catalog
database, or just a catalog of

00:25:04.570 --> 00:25:07.906
stuff that's in the sky and the
last 10 petabytes of actually

00:25:07.906 --> 00:25:13.212
the structured data; of course,
NCAR is an NSF funded center.

00:25:13.212 --> 00:25:15.914
They do a lot of
observational data and they

00:25:15.914 --> 00:25:17.182
also do simulations.

00:25:17.182 --> 00:25:21.086
They have a new super computer
that's able to do bigger and

00:25:21.086 --> 00:25:24.356
bigger simulations and
generate more and more data.

00:25:25.991 --> 00:25:31.330
There are a couple of other
observational projects at NSF.

00:25:31.330 --> 00:25:35.401
One is EarthScope in the Geo
sciences that's essentially

00:25:35.401 --> 00:25:40.439
trying to do a 40-snapshot
of the lithospheric structure

00:25:40.439 --> 00:25:41.607
of continental U.S.

00:25:41.607 --> 00:25:45.043
So, they have seismograms and
other instruments that have

00:25:45.043 --> 00:25:48.514
swept across the continent
and collected all this data.

00:25:48.514 --> 00:25:52.317
And there's the Ocean Observing
Initiative collecting data

00:25:52.317 --> 00:25:54.052
from ocean sites.

00:25:55.854 --> 00:25:58.057
There are other new things
coming along the way.

00:25:58.057 --> 00:26:00.859
You might have heard about this
interest in something called the

00:26:00.859 --> 00:26:04.129
Materials Genome Initiative,
which is more in the chemistry

00:26:04.129 --> 00:26:05.264
and material sciences.

00:26:05.264 --> 00:26:08.100
That's a very nascent area.

00:26:08.100 --> 00:26:10.936
In fact, that community is
trying to figure out what their

00:26:10.936 --> 00:26:16.008
data challenges really are going
to be and one of the things that

00:26:16.008 --> 00:26:19.878
they encounter is
heterogeneity of data.

00:26:19.878 --> 00:26:23.282
Right now, the amounts of
data may not be large but they

00:26:23.282 --> 00:26:26.919
collect all sorts of different
kinds of data and the idea here

00:26:26.919 --> 00:26:31.757
is to see what you can do by
data collection to speed up the

00:26:31.757 --> 00:26:34.927
materials, discover and
smart... eventually leading to

00:26:34.927 --> 00:26:37.729
things like smart
manufacturing and so on.

00:26:39.198 --> 00:26:41.700
All of these projects,
as I've mentioned before,

00:26:41.700 --> 00:26:43.902
are instrumentation projects.

00:26:43.902 --> 00:26:47.439
They get funded through the
special mechanisms that NSF has

00:26:47.439 --> 00:26:54.313
for one-time allocation of large
chunks of money that go towards

00:26:54.313 --> 00:26:57.316
building these things, so
these are called the MREFC,

00:26:57.316 --> 00:27:01.053
Major Research Equipment and
Facility Construction awards.

00:27:01.053 --> 00:27:06.158
Usually in the few hundred
million dollar range,

00:27:06.158 --> 00:27:07.926
but I think this term
was also mentioned today.

00:27:07.926 --> 00:27:09.628
Somebody talked
about data scope.

00:27:09.628 --> 00:27:11.396
Maybe it was Bob,
I can't remember now.

00:27:11.396 --> 00:27:15.701
Data, in some sense, is the new
instrument, so if I have a lot

00:27:15.701 --> 00:27:19.872
of data available to me, like
this data scientist at Facebook

00:27:19.872 --> 00:27:23.041
who goes to work every day
and the petabytes of stuff that

00:27:23.041 --> 00:27:26.044
we're doing in terms of click
streams and images that we

00:27:26.044 --> 00:27:29.414
loaded up in Facebook,
that's their data.

00:27:29.414 --> 00:27:34.586
Increasingly, we're going to go
to a mode of operation where

00:27:34.586 --> 00:27:38.390
just analyzing that data
is the work that you do.

00:27:38.390 --> 00:27:42.361
So, it's not as much on the
collection side, it's more on

00:27:42.361 --> 00:27:43.195
the analysis side.

00:27:43.195 --> 00:27:45.664
I'm going to come back and
talk about that actually as

00:27:45.664 --> 00:27:48.200
one of our challenges.

00:27:48.200 --> 00:27:51.703
How do we make sure that, as
a community, we actually have

00:27:51.703 --> 00:27:53.171
access to more and more data?

00:27:53.171 --> 00:27:57.042
That's going to be
a bit of a challenge.

00:27:58.477 --> 00:27:59.811
This is very quick.

00:27:59.811 --> 00:28:01.947
I think some of you
would be very familiar.

00:28:01.947 --> 00:28:06.585
I'm in the CAC observatories, I
think jointly run by Yale which

00:28:06.585 --> 00:28:09.321
was up in Mauna Kea and I
was at the VLDB Conference.

00:28:09.321 --> 00:28:11.523
I don't know if any of you had
gone there, was just a couple of

00:28:11.523 --> 00:28:15.127
weeks ago on the big island, and
I actually went to Mauna Kea,

00:28:15.127 --> 00:28:19.231
first time in my life,
and saw some of these.

00:28:19.231 --> 00:28:24.603
Basically, what these
communities are facing that

00:28:24.603 --> 00:28:28.574
increasingly all of the rest of
science is facing, is they're

00:28:28.574 --> 00:28:32.344
able to easily collect data
which is much more than all the

00:28:32.344 --> 00:28:36.081
data you had so far in
the history of the field.

00:28:36.081 --> 00:28:39.585
So, in the case of astronomy,
within a decade over 140

00:28:39.585 --> 00:28:44.656
terabytes of information was
collected so far before Sloan

00:28:44.656 --> 00:28:49.628
Digital Sky Survey, which is
already a decade or more old,

00:28:49.628 --> 00:28:53.532
and within the first few weeks
the Sloan Survey all took that.

00:28:53.532 --> 00:28:58.270
LSST, in the first 10 days or so
overtakes what Sloan collected.

00:28:58.270 --> 00:29:02.941
So, a huge rates at which
these data are being generated.

00:29:02.941 --> 00:29:07.546
Now, if you step back and think
in terms of where we are in all

00:29:07.546 --> 00:29:14.286
of this as an R&amp;D discipline or
R&amp;D activity, at the bottom what

00:29:14.286 --> 00:29:18.190
I try to show with my funky
little diagram is, you know,

00:29:18.190 --> 00:29:22.694
so far what we've been looking
at in the data area is building

00:29:22.694 --> 00:29:25.897
data acquisition systems
that can collect lots of data,

00:29:25.897 --> 00:29:31.570
building data repositories that
can serve community data sets.

00:29:31.570 --> 00:29:34.973
Perhaps developing knowledge
structures, anthologies or

00:29:34.973 --> 00:29:39.411
semantics of the information so
that they can be proper reuse

00:29:39.411 --> 00:29:41.446
and use of the data.

00:29:41.446 --> 00:29:44.516
Ideas like the data commons
so that data can be shared.

00:29:44.516 --> 00:29:48.954
So, I can think of all of those
as sort of the infrastructure

00:29:48.954 --> 00:29:52.124
that's around data and a lot
of what we have done so far

00:29:52.124 --> 00:29:53.959
is in that area.

00:29:56.094 --> 00:29:59.264
The research programs that NSF
is funding right now, in fact,

00:29:59.264 --> 00:30:02.034
our big data program is actually
called Critical Techniques and

00:30:02.034 --> 00:30:06.872
Technologies for Big Data,
I think are the next level up,

00:30:06.872 --> 00:30:10.909
which is you already have these
building blocks, how do you now

00:30:10.909 --> 00:30:15.681
build systems that allow you to
make use of data in a much more

00:30:15.681 --> 00:30:19.084
facile and easy way, or maybe
develop new algorithms on the

00:30:19.084 --> 00:30:20.452
data that you have?

00:30:20.452 --> 00:30:25.557
And some of this speaks to this
idea of the human data science

00:30:25.557 --> 00:30:28.093
interface and, I think again,
Bob mentioned that.

00:30:28.093 --> 00:30:32.798
So, you could either the
horizontal bar, meaning that you

00:30:32.798 --> 00:30:37.502
could build technologies that
take existing pieces and put

00:30:37.502 --> 00:30:40.739
them together and make them
much more usable, maybe you

00:30:40.739 --> 00:30:42.607
build workflows, etcetera.

00:30:42.607 --> 00:30:45.610
The vertical piece meaning that
we might be inventing new ways

00:30:45.610 --> 00:30:48.480
of doing things that we may have
to go back and change some of

00:30:48.480 --> 00:30:51.016
what's going on
in the technologies.

00:30:51.016 --> 00:30:53.318
So, Hadoop was mentioned
earlier, so if you look at

00:30:53.318 --> 00:30:55.987
something like Hadoop,
people are, in a way,

00:30:55.987 --> 00:30:58.990
reengineering Hadoop to
make it much more online.

00:30:58.990 --> 00:31:01.593
So, when Hadoop first started
off it was kind of more for

00:31:01.593 --> 00:31:04.796
batch oriented processing
paradigm, but people want to

00:31:04.796 --> 00:31:08.166
process these huge amounts of
data using more of an online

00:31:08.166 --> 00:31:10.402
paradigm, so maybe we have
to go and reengineer some

00:31:10.402 --> 00:31:11.470
of those things.

00:31:11.470 --> 00:31:12.504
So, that's the kind of idea.

00:31:12.504 --> 00:31:15.640
So, I think of what's down below
and what we have done so far as

00:31:15.640 --> 00:31:19.111
the first generation of big data
and I think where we need to be

00:31:19.111 --> 00:31:21.513
heading is the second
generation, which is actually

00:31:21.513 --> 00:31:26.351
the use of the data for useful
things, whether it's in science

00:31:26.351 --> 00:31:29.020
or societal applications
or business applications.

00:31:29.020 --> 00:31:33.358
So, it's the application of this
data towards some objective is

00:31:33.358 --> 00:31:34.993
really the target.

00:31:37.162 --> 00:31:39.498
So, what are the
challenges in doing this?

00:31:39.498 --> 00:31:43.635
How do you promote and incentive
interdisciplinary research?

00:31:43.635 --> 00:31:48.373
I think just like you folks are
doing here, I think - I don't

00:31:48.373 --> 00:31:52.077
know if I can say all - but many
campuses are facing this issue.

00:31:52.077 --> 00:31:54.946
How do you bring folks
together on campus?

00:31:54.946 --> 00:31:59.117
If you really think about it,
people's life on campuses also

00:31:59.117 --> 00:32:01.253
hurdle at light speed.

00:32:01.253 --> 00:32:02.521
Everybody has stuff to do.

00:32:02.521 --> 00:32:05.056
They have to publish their next
paper, graduate the next tour

00:32:05.056 --> 00:32:08.360
and teach the next class and
then we are asking them to do

00:32:08.360 --> 00:32:12.597
this interdisciplinary thing so
there's a challenge here and how

00:32:12.597 --> 00:32:14.766
do you incentivize that
is one of the challenges.

00:32:14.766 --> 00:32:18.003
One way, we think, would be
through research programs that

00:32:18.003 --> 00:32:20.205
require you to interact.

00:32:21.573 --> 00:32:24.009
Another challenge, as I
mentioned, and I'll be coming

00:32:24.009 --> 00:32:29.915
back to this theme, is how do we
provide access to researchers,

00:32:29.915 --> 00:32:32.184
to interesting and real data?

00:32:32.184 --> 00:32:34.586
I think that's a real challenge.

00:32:34.586 --> 00:32:39.257
It's at the crux of breaking
some of these bottlenecks and

00:32:39.257 --> 00:32:41.193
getting more interesting
results out of data.

00:32:41.193 --> 00:32:44.729
Again, if you think in terms of
Generation 1 and Generation 2

00:32:44.729 --> 00:32:48.033
that I just showed, we are still
sort of in the Generation 1

00:32:48.033 --> 00:32:49.701
where we're collecting
lots of data.

00:32:49.701 --> 00:32:52.404
We would like to move to
Generation 2 where the

00:32:52.404 --> 00:32:53.405
data is available.

00:32:53.405 --> 00:32:56.575
And what you should really be
doing is using your smarts to

00:32:56.575 --> 00:32:59.611
analyze the data and do
something interesting with it.

00:32:59.611 --> 00:33:02.914
So, how do we get
there is a challenge.

00:33:02.914 --> 00:33:06.184
Another challenge I see is how
do we provide the platforms,

00:33:06.184 --> 00:33:09.888
and this is, I'm talking about
in academia, that allow you to

00:33:09.888 --> 00:33:11.189
do data science research.

00:33:11.189 --> 00:33:14.159
And when I say platform, I mean
the hardware, the software,

00:33:14.159 --> 00:33:14.826
and the data.

00:33:14.826 --> 00:33:16.962
They're all part
of the platform.

00:33:16.962 --> 00:33:19.364
There are different parts of the
research community that might

00:33:19.364 --> 00:33:22.901
just want a big hardware
platform so maybe they can dial

00:33:22.901 --> 00:33:26.037
up the next hadoop or
next something, right?

00:33:26.037 --> 00:33:29.040
There are others who want
to use the available software

00:33:29.040 --> 00:33:30.775
to do some analysis.

00:33:30.775 --> 00:33:33.745
One of the things I've heard
from the community certainly is

00:33:33.745 --> 00:33:38.483
the data science, databased type
communities, a lot of papers are

00:33:38.483 --> 00:33:42.354
published about results,
you know, how good, how fast

00:33:42.354 --> 00:33:44.856
something runs, and there
are many such papers.

00:33:44.856 --> 00:33:48.393
Every paper uses its own system
and these papers are really,

00:33:48.393 --> 00:33:50.962
at some level, not compatible
because they're all running on

00:33:50.962 --> 00:33:55.834
different hardware doing their
own, essentially, assumptions.

00:33:55.834 --> 00:33:58.703
And so, if you really start
getting bothered by that then

00:33:58.703 --> 00:34:01.907
you say, well, could we create
some kind of a platform on which

00:34:01.907 --> 00:34:05.644
we can have compatible results?

00:34:05.644 --> 00:34:09.614
And finally, the last one, which
is right now, at least to me,

00:34:09.614 --> 00:34:12.684
seems the holy grail, is there
are researchers, at least we

00:34:12.684 --> 00:34:14.653
would like them, to
just work with the data.

00:34:14.653 --> 00:34:17.489
So, I give you the platform,
I give you all the software,

00:34:17.489 --> 00:34:20.258
what I want you to do is do
something interesting with the

00:34:20.258 --> 00:34:22.961
data and this could either
be generating new algorithms

00:34:22.961 --> 00:34:28.633
or coming up with maybe new
science or new ways of making

00:34:28.633 --> 00:34:29.668
decisions with the data.

00:34:29.668 --> 00:34:31.970
So, all three are
part of the platform.

00:34:31.970 --> 00:34:37.042
The reason this is a challenge
is, you may have heard of this,

00:34:37.042 --> 00:34:39.377
and people are now calling
this the brain drain, right?

00:34:39.377 --> 00:34:43.081
I've been in discussions where
there's a concern of the brain

00:34:43.081 --> 00:34:46.484
drain, that is researchers
leaving academia to essentially

00:34:46.484 --> 00:34:49.888
go to Google and Microsoft
and Facebook because that's -

00:34:49.888 --> 00:34:51.156
why are they doing it?

00:34:51.156 --> 00:34:52.590
Because that's
where the data is.

00:34:52.590 --> 00:34:54.726
So, if you really want to
do interesting stuff with

00:34:54.726 --> 00:34:57.429
real data, people are
moving off to those places.

00:34:57.429 --> 00:35:00.799
Certainly, I know at UC-San
Diego we lost several of our

00:35:00.799 --> 00:35:05.236
colleagues; one to Google, one
to Amazon, one to Microsoft.

00:35:06.304 --> 00:35:08.840
And if you're a systems
person that's where the

00:35:08.840 --> 00:35:10.575
big platforms are.

00:35:10.575 --> 00:35:13.511
So, I think this is something
we need to address and how

00:35:13.511 --> 00:35:14.713
do we do this?

00:35:14.713 --> 00:35:17.882
So, maybe this is my chance
to do the soap box on this one.

00:35:17.882 --> 00:35:23.088
A few decades ago the thing that
universities couldn't get their

00:35:23.088 --> 00:35:24.756
hands on was a super computer.

00:35:24.756 --> 00:35:27.792
The most any university could
buy a super computer like a

00:35:27.792 --> 00:35:32.697
Cray, the one that Bob showed in
his slides, the circular thing.

00:35:32.697 --> 00:35:34.799
So, therefore, there was a
national program that said, gee,

00:35:34.799 --> 00:35:37.002
we'd better buy these things
and make them available

00:35:37.002 --> 00:35:38.036
to the community.

00:35:38.036 --> 00:35:41.006
Today, what most single
university can buy is a cluster

00:35:41.006 --> 00:35:45.176
that Google could have,
100 or cluster.

00:35:45.176 --> 00:35:47.245
So, how do you facilitate that?

00:35:47.245 --> 00:35:51.950
So, this is, I have certainly
started with folks in the

00:35:51.950 --> 00:35:54.819
community who are very
interested in at least thinking

00:35:54.819 --> 00:35:58.256
about how to go about this
problem so maybe some of you

00:35:58.256 --> 00:36:00.825
will pick up on that.

00:36:00.825 --> 00:36:04.295
Education and development of
a new pedagogy in data science,

00:36:04.295 --> 00:36:06.031
and I'll come back
and talk about that.

00:36:06.031 --> 00:36:10.301
So, data science becomes a thing
unto itself and so how do we

00:36:10.301 --> 00:36:13.672
develop education programs and
what are the principles of data

00:36:13.672 --> 00:36:17.942
science as distinct from
computer science on one side

00:36:17.942 --> 00:36:21.579
and statistics
on the other side.

00:36:21.579 --> 00:36:24.082
Sustainability of resources,
another big problem.

00:36:24.082 --> 00:36:27.118
So, those of you who are running
campus resources you know that

00:36:27.118 --> 00:36:30.288
we have data management plans
but how do you keep this going?

00:36:30.288 --> 00:36:35.393
Reproducibility of research
is another big issue, and I was

00:36:35.393 --> 00:36:38.530
mentioning to someone,
the director of NSF just had a

00:36:38.530 --> 00:36:42.534
seminar a couple of days ago, a
one-day workshop on this issue

00:36:42.534 --> 00:36:46.938
of reproducibility and I
think it's a big, big deal.

00:36:46.938 --> 00:36:49.107
If you want, we can
certainly get into discussions.

00:36:49.107 --> 00:36:53.078
I won't say anything more about
that and I already mentioned the

00:36:53.078 --> 00:36:55.313
brain drain issue.

00:36:55.313 --> 00:36:56.781
So, let's see...

00:37:00.285 --> 00:37:03.254
I'm trying to see how
I can liven this up.

00:37:05.490 --> 00:37:11.296
Well, I guess I just
have to go through it.

00:37:11.296 --> 00:37:15.066
This is the 30,000 foot level
thing I was talking about.

00:37:15.066 --> 00:37:19.037
So, if you think in terms of
the investments that NSF does,

00:37:19.037 --> 00:37:24.042
and actually, this would apply
to any federal agency that's

00:37:24.042 --> 00:37:28.246
doing R&amp;D investments, this is a
reasonable quadrant diagram that

00:37:28.246 --> 00:37:30.515
we all have started
using to describe that.

00:37:30.515 --> 00:37:33.551
So, in one box you have
foundational research; on the

00:37:33.551 --> 00:37:35.320
other side you have
cyberinfrastructure,

00:37:35.320 --> 00:37:39.190
the facilities; there's
education and training; and then

00:37:39.190 --> 00:37:41.860
there's, in a way, it's
something which we call

00:37:41.860 --> 00:37:44.729
community building and the
reason that's important in

00:37:44.729 --> 00:37:48.233
big data is, again, because
of what we said before.

00:37:48.233 --> 00:37:51.803
This is a field where we are
trying to connect technologies,

00:37:51.803 --> 00:37:54.939
and I don't mean just computer
science, it's statistics, math,

00:37:54.939 --> 00:37:59.744
CS... all of those... with end
application, real applications.

00:37:59.744 --> 00:38:04.482
And that requires
forming collaborations.

00:38:04.482 --> 00:38:08.620
It also requires collaborations
that are not just among academic

00:38:08.620 --> 00:38:12.624
institutions but what we call
multi sector, so academia,

00:38:12.624 --> 00:38:15.827
industry, and government,
and it's government at

00:38:15.827 --> 00:38:18.496
all levels... local,
state, federal.

00:38:18.496 --> 00:38:23.067
So, that's an important part
of trying to accelerate sort of

00:38:23.067 --> 00:38:24.702
the whole ecosystem.

00:38:24.702 --> 00:38:26.638
And in the middle sits policy.

00:38:26.638 --> 00:38:30.575
Again, somebody mentioned data
policy, I think maybe in your

00:38:30.575 --> 00:38:35.547
talk, NSF has put out a data
policy and so these are all

00:38:35.547 --> 00:38:38.850
things that govern all of this.

00:38:38.850 --> 00:38:43.254
So, quickly, in foundational
research, big data is a program

00:38:43.254 --> 00:38:44.389
out of NSF.

00:38:44.389 --> 00:38:46.124
We've just announced the awards.

00:38:46.124 --> 00:38:47.592
I have some numbers later on.

00:38:47.592 --> 00:38:52.797
I think we funded 30 proposals
this year in big data.

00:38:52.797 --> 00:38:54.332
It's a cross-fitting initiative.

00:38:54.332 --> 00:38:58.636
It involves all directorates at
NSF, it also involves an agency

00:38:58.636 --> 00:39:01.439
called the Office of Financial
Research, and I might have

00:39:01.439 --> 00:39:06.244
something later on that, but
Wafer was set up through the

00:39:06.244 --> 00:39:10.381
Department of Treasury and their
task is, essentially, to build a

00:39:10.381 --> 00:39:13.151
model of the financial system
of the country so that they

00:39:13.151 --> 00:39:16.955
can try to do some
predictive models of where

00:39:16.955 --> 00:39:18.923
the financial system is going.

00:39:18.923 --> 00:39:22.160
They have access to very
interesting data and they also

00:39:22.160 --> 00:39:26.898
have access to extremely
fine-grained financial data that

00:39:26.898 --> 00:39:32.971
is confidential, but researchers
can get access to it in the NBA

00:39:32.971 --> 00:39:35.974
sort of way, so it's sort of
like working with census data if

00:39:35.974 --> 00:39:37.575
you're a demographer.

00:39:37.575 --> 00:39:40.078
You can get micro data out
of census, but there are some

00:39:40.078 --> 00:39:41.446
rules and regulations.

00:39:41.446 --> 00:39:44.849
So, OFR is the same.

00:39:44.849 --> 00:39:48.119
We also have a program called
CDS&amp;E, Computational Data

00:39:48.119 --> 00:39:50.188
Science &amp; Engineering.

00:39:50.188 --> 00:39:52.790
The distinction between these
two is in big data we are really

00:39:52.790 --> 00:39:57.428
looking for an edge that makes
it new and different because of

00:39:57.428 --> 00:39:58.463
the big phenomena.

00:39:58.463 --> 00:40:00.732
Again, it's all those V's
that you saw in the slide.

00:40:00.732 --> 00:40:03.901
You know, they have lots of
data, scaling is an issue, or if

00:40:03.901 --> 00:40:07.005
you have lots of data maybe
simpler models work better than

00:40:07.005 --> 00:40:09.874
more complex models, or actually
complex models won't work

00:40:09.874 --> 00:40:12.777
because they're just too
computationally intensive.

00:40:12.777 --> 00:40:15.880
Maybe you have a variety of
data so a lot of heterogeneity

00:40:15.880 --> 00:40:16.347
in the data.

00:40:16.347 --> 00:40:18.549
Maybe it's semantic
heterogeneity or some other

00:40:18.549 --> 00:40:20.251
form of heterogeneity.

00:40:20.251 --> 00:40:23.588
So, it has to be something in
the big data solicitation where

00:40:23.588 --> 00:40:27.959
it's pushing a new aspect
of data, whereas in CDS&amp;E,

00:40:27.959 --> 00:40:31.062
the focus is more on your
pushing a new science.

00:40:31.062 --> 00:40:34.732
You may be using a standard,
a graph method that we saw

00:40:34.732 --> 00:40:38.336
earlier, you know, there may be
some complexity in the graph as

00:40:38.336 --> 00:40:41.205
to what the nodes mean and what
the links mean, but what you are

00:40:41.205 --> 00:40:43.741
really pushing is the
science that comes out of it.

00:40:43.741 --> 00:40:46.577
So, that's sort of the
distinction between the two.

00:40:46.577 --> 00:40:48.513
And we also have
some special programs.

00:40:48.513 --> 00:40:50.915
Big Data for Disasters
was a program that was

00:40:50.915 --> 00:40:52.550
done with Japan.

00:40:52.550 --> 00:40:56.988
There's one called FutureCloud,
which is sort of a largish scale

00:40:56.988 --> 00:41:00.658
platform, but it's much more
focused on really sort of

00:41:00.658 --> 00:41:04.028
operating systems and network
research and not so much on the

00:41:04.028 --> 00:41:07.165
data research itself.

00:41:07.165 --> 00:41:11.002
And FutureCloud has been
awarded, I think there are two

00:41:11.002 --> 00:41:12.904
platforms in the country
which are meant to

00:41:12.904 --> 00:41:15.039
be community resources.

00:41:15.039 --> 00:41:17.008
Cyberinfrastructure, I
think you know about this.

00:41:17.008 --> 00:41:18.976
The ACI was mentioned before.

00:41:18.976 --> 00:41:20.812
There is CC*DNI.

00:41:20.812 --> 00:41:23.247
I think I heard Yale must -
you've got one of these 100

00:41:23.247 --> 00:41:28.619
gigabyte awards to connect the
campus, so these are for high

00:41:28.619 --> 00:41:30.955
speed networking,
building data resources,

00:41:30.955 --> 00:41:34.392
and for HBC platforms.

00:41:34.392 --> 00:41:38.696
I mentioned NRT on the education
side, and I'll come back to tell

00:41:38.696 --> 00:41:41.833
you a little bit more of what
we are doing on education.

00:41:41.833 --> 00:41:45.636
And one of the things that we
have started for the community

00:41:45.636 --> 00:41:48.639
building is this new
program called Big Data

00:41:48.639 --> 00:41:50.608
Regional Innovation Hubs.

00:41:52.577 --> 00:41:57.982
Those awards were just recently
made, I believe, and I'll say

00:41:57.982 --> 00:42:01.986
more about it, so let
me keep quiet for now.

00:42:01.986 --> 00:42:05.990
And all of this is in the
context of all of the policy

00:42:05.990 --> 00:42:08.393
issues that are coming out,
everything from the DMP.

00:42:08.393 --> 00:42:14.732
You have the DMP2, to open
data policies, etcetera.

00:42:14.732 --> 00:42:19.003
So, as I mentioned before, the
Big Data program has a focus

00:42:19.003 --> 00:42:22.707
both on foundational research
and on innovative applications.

00:42:22.707 --> 00:42:24.342
So, it's just a
question of the dial.

00:42:24.342 --> 00:42:28.246
The foundational research - in
both cases you are looking for

00:42:28.246 --> 00:42:31.182
an application that
motivates the research, but in

00:42:31.182 --> 00:42:34.185
foundational, you are really
pushing some new techniques and

00:42:34.185 --> 00:42:38.589
technologies, again, whether
in CS, statistics, or math.

00:42:38.589 --> 00:42:41.526
In Innovative Applications, we
are looking for something new

00:42:41.526 --> 00:42:46.197
being done in the science,
but also using some innovative

00:42:46.197 --> 00:42:48.299
techniques with the data.

00:42:48.299 --> 00:42:50.501
And, as I mentioned,
OFR is a participant.

00:42:50.501 --> 00:42:54.539
All of this, by the way, got
started with the announcement by

00:42:54.539 --> 00:42:57.275
the Obama Administration for
the Big Data R&amp;D Initiative,

00:42:57.275 --> 00:43:01.612
which was in 2012, I think,
so, 2013 was the first set

00:43:01.612 --> 00:43:06.050
of awards, when NSF and NIH
were together at that time in

00:43:06.050 --> 00:43:10.288
the program and that's
just a breakdown of...

00:43:13.724 --> 00:43:15.893
I think you can sort of see,
there's purple on one side,

00:43:15.893 --> 00:43:18.362
that's all the proposals
that we're focusing on

00:43:18.362 --> 00:43:20.098
data collection,
management, mining,

00:43:20.098 --> 00:43:22.033
and machine learning.

00:43:23.034 --> 00:43:26.304
The darker one is health and bio
informatics, and then there are

00:43:26.304 --> 00:43:31.008
other focus areas like social
networks, physical science,

00:43:31.008 --> 00:43:33.678
engineering,
foundations, and CI.

00:43:34.745 --> 00:43:38.149
In 2014, we had a slightly
different categorization.

00:43:38.149 --> 00:43:41.819
There's four categories:
Data and Knowledge Analytics;

00:43:41.819 --> 00:43:45.122
Data and Knowledge Management;
Computational Scientific

00:43:45.122 --> 00:43:48.626
Discovery; and
Innovative Applications.

00:43:48.626 --> 00:43:50.394
And you see the breakdown there.

00:43:50.394 --> 00:43:52.697
There were 35 projects funded.

00:43:52.697 --> 00:43:56.267
And the most recent one that's
just come out, and you can go to

00:43:56.267 --> 00:43:59.971
FastLane and search and look for
big data and the current awards

00:43:59.971 --> 00:44:01.873
we have funded.

00:44:01.873 --> 00:44:07.912
42 proposals, which are 30
different projects and that's

00:44:07.912 --> 00:44:10.448
because several of
them are collaborative

00:44:10.448 --> 00:44:12.550
across multiple universities.

00:44:13.584 --> 00:44:16.120
Most of them are actually
collaborative, at least within

00:44:16.120 --> 00:44:18.623
the same campus, okay?

00:44:18.623 --> 00:44:20.158
And, here's the breakdown.

00:44:20.158 --> 00:44:24.795
63% were on the Foundational
side, 20% on the Innovative

00:44:24.795 --> 00:44:28.432
Applications, and 17% were
a combination of the two.

00:44:28.432 --> 00:44:31.669
And when I say foundational,
again, it's computer science,

00:44:31.669 --> 00:44:36.774
statistics, and math, so
Stats and Math directorate is

00:44:36.774 --> 00:44:39.443
a participant in
the big data program.

00:44:39.443 --> 00:44:42.313
And there are 6 EAGER
grants that were also funded

00:44:42.313 --> 00:44:44.015
as part of this.

00:44:45.816 --> 00:44:50.655
So, the Hubs and Spokes, that's
a new program and the basic idea

00:44:50.655 --> 00:44:53.624
here, and I won't belabor all
the details, but the basic idea

00:44:53.624 --> 00:44:57.094
is that we see an opportunity.

00:44:57.094 --> 00:45:00.398
I think, at the end of the day,
we are trying to push towards

00:45:00.398 --> 00:45:05.136
how to A) bring more data out
and make it available for the

00:45:05.136 --> 00:45:09.907
community to use in their
research; and, B) how to do this

00:45:09.907 --> 00:45:14.779
by creating multi
sector partnerships.

00:45:14.779 --> 00:45:22.687
So, the way the program ended
up is that we have this notion

00:45:22.687 --> 00:45:25.723
of hubs, which are these
coordinating groups that are

00:45:25.723 --> 00:45:30.094
meant to coordinate within
regions and we had a series of

00:45:30.094 --> 00:45:35.266
meetings to plan this program,
and something very interesting

00:45:35.266 --> 00:45:36.634
came up in these meetings.

00:45:36.634 --> 00:45:42.406
The meetings were in each of the
regions, so, we have the west,

00:45:42.406 --> 00:45:45.176
northeast, midwest, and south.

00:45:45.176 --> 00:45:47.111
These are the four regions.

00:45:47.111 --> 00:45:49.614
And when we had these meetings
there were a lot of universities

00:45:49.614 --> 00:45:53.451
from those regions
as well as industry.

00:45:53.451 --> 00:45:55.953
A lot of the discussion was
about how these people are

00:45:55.953 --> 00:45:58.556
coming together for the first
time and one of the things we

00:45:58.556 --> 00:46:01.726
asked in these meetings is ask
people to put up what resources

00:46:01.726 --> 00:46:03.294
do they have available.

00:46:03.294 --> 00:46:04.795
And all of a sudden,
the universities started

00:46:04.795 --> 00:46:05.463
saying, oh, really?

00:46:05.463 --> 00:46:06.631
You guys have this data?

00:46:06.631 --> 00:46:08.099
We didn't even know
that you had that.

00:46:08.099 --> 00:46:12.703
And industry was talking about
what kind of requirements they

00:46:12.703 --> 00:46:15.139
have and the universities hadn't
heard from the local industry.

00:46:15.139 --> 00:46:18.042
So, there's actually an
interesting sort of synergy

00:46:18.042 --> 00:46:21.345
beginning to happen within the
regions and I'm actually very

00:46:21.345 --> 00:46:24.849
hopeful that this is going to be
a very interesting mechanism by

00:46:24.849 --> 00:46:28.419
which new ideas will come
forward and new collaborations

00:46:28.419 --> 00:46:29.654
will come forward.

00:46:29.654 --> 00:46:33.691
One of the things we are looking
for the Hubs to do, actually the

00:46:33.691 --> 00:46:37.028
next program logically after
the hubs, is called the Spokes,

00:46:37.028 --> 00:46:39.597
and right now, we are working
on the solicitation for that.

00:46:39.597 --> 00:46:43.668
We expect, in a month or so,
maybe a couple of months,

00:46:43.668 --> 00:46:46.003
the solicitation will come out.

00:46:46.003 --> 00:46:49.473
As I mentioned, the Hubs were
meant to be this very high level

00:46:49.473 --> 00:46:52.076
coordinating activities
in the region.

00:46:52.076 --> 00:46:56.013
The Spokes are meant to have
a similar kind of diversity of

00:46:56.013 --> 00:47:00.017
participation, but focus
on specific technical areas.

00:47:00.017 --> 00:47:03.254
So, when the Hubs got together,
there were a lot of different

00:47:03.254 --> 00:47:05.489
themes that came up
that they identified.

00:47:05.489 --> 00:47:09.560
One theme that seems to be
common across the whole country

00:47:09.560 --> 00:47:12.330
is urban science
and smart cities.

00:47:12.330 --> 00:47:14.999
So many regions are
interested in issues related

00:47:14.999 --> 00:47:16.333
to smart cities.

00:47:16.333 --> 00:47:21.205
Another is global climate change
and, for example, in the west,

00:47:21.205 --> 00:47:22.440
water was a big issue.

00:47:22.440 --> 00:47:24.809
So, climate change and water.

00:47:24.809 --> 00:47:29.013
In the midwest, managing the
fresh water in the Great Lakes

00:47:29.013 --> 00:47:30.147
was a big issue.

00:47:30.147 --> 00:47:31.982
So, there were some
common themes of this type.

00:47:31.982 --> 00:47:37.455
And, of course, personal health
was an issue for everybody.

00:47:37.455 --> 00:47:40.858
So, what we want the Spokes
to do, then, is to zoom into

00:47:40.858 --> 00:47:45.930
specific areas of that type and
the Spokes will be affiliated

00:47:45.930 --> 00:47:48.599
with the Hubs, but they
can have membership from

00:47:48.599 --> 00:47:49.366
across the country.

00:47:49.366 --> 00:47:51.001
They don't have to
be all members within

00:47:51.001 --> 00:47:52.803
the particular region.

00:47:52.803 --> 00:47:56.707
So, that's the next solicitation
that's going to be coming out.

00:47:58.809 --> 00:48:01.312
So, the idea of the Hubs
is basically to initiate

00:48:01.312 --> 00:48:05.282
partnerships; identify what
might be common resources in the

00:48:05.282 --> 00:48:08.119
region, so maybe you have a
super computer, a center or

00:48:08.119 --> 00:48:10.588
storage facility here, maybe
there's a university down the

00:48:10.588 --> 00:48:13.691
street that would like to use
it, but they have something else

00:48:13.691 --> 00:48:17.995
to form these kind of
collaborations; access to the

00:48:17.995 --> 00:48:24.168
talent, both on the technology
side and the science side;

00:48:24.168 --> 00:48:26.403
and then, shared best practices.

00:48:26.403 --> 00:48:30.341
So, as I said, one of the things
we'd like these Hubs to do is to

00:48:30.341 --> 00:48:33.544
start identifying datasets
that may be available and

00:48:33.544 --> 00:48:35.146
expose this data.

00:48:35.146 --> 00:48:38.349
Again, you already heard about
something like the NCI Commons,

00:48:38.349 --> 00:48:41.886
which in a way, is actually
doing it for cancer data,

00:48:41.886 --> 00:48:43.320
and I think Bob did mention it.

00:48:43.320 --> 00:48:46.090
So, as you start doing this, you
start running into this issue of

00:48:46.090 --> 00:48:48.726
API's and standards because
I want to share this data with

00:48:48.726 --> 00:48:52.263
that guy, and so, what are
the standard things by which

00:48:52.263 --> 00:48:53.430
we can do this?

00:48:53.430 --> 00:48:56.801
NSF has funded an activity
called RDA, the Research Data

00:48:56.801 --> 00:48:59.603
Alliance, which is trying
to do some of these kinds

00:48:59.603 --> 00:49:03.307
of standards development.

00:49:03.307 --> 00:49:08.012
And so, we'd like the Hubs
to play a role in not only just

00:49:08.012 --> 00:49:10.848
trying to identify the data but
also trying to identify along

00:49:10.848 --> 00:49:13.684
what standards can
these be made available.

00:49:17.054 --> 00:49:19.757
And, by doing this, you know,
by the region, we hopefully

00:49:19.757 --> 00:49:23.260
reduce the cost of, and sort of
reduce some of the duplication

00:49:23.260 --> 00:49:24.962
that happens in these things.

00:49:24.962 --> 00:49:27.331
So, we had these regional
meetings, as I mentioned.

00:49:27.331 --> 00:49:31.135
The midwest folks, this
was several months ago,

00:49:31.135 --> 00:49:32.102
they met in Ann Arbor.

00:49:32.102 --> 00:49:35.372
The west folks actually
met in Salt Lake City.

00:49:35.372 --> 00:49:40.244
The northeast meeting was in
Boston, but I think there's a

00:49:40.244 --> 00:49:44.782
group at NYU that was trying to
put together the northeast Hub.

00:49:44.782 --> 00:49:47.351
These awards have
been announced.

00:49:47.351 --> 00:49:49.854
I think you can find them
on FastLane but they're not

00:49:49.854 --> 00:49:52.456
yet officially announced.

00:49:52.456 --> 00:49:58.062
And the south, the meeting was
in North Carolina and RENCI,

00:49:58.062 --> 00:50:00.998
which is University of
North Carolina Chapel Hill

00:50:00.998 --> 00:50:06.103
and Georgia Tech were
involved with that.

00:50:07.504 --> 00:50:09.139
I already mentioned this.

00:50:09.139 --> 00:50:12.743
These are some of the topics
that came up in the different

00:50:12.743 --> 00:50:15.880
Hubs and what we found is,
it's interesting, some topics

00:50:15.880 --> 00:50:19.283
are of interest for specific
regions, there are others that

00:50:19.283 --> 00:50:23.854
cut across all four sectors.

00:50:23.854 --> 00:50:27.458
So, some quick updates and
I should be done after that.

00:50:27.458 --> 00:50:32.062
Let me talk about some
recent and upcoming activities.

00:50:32.062 --> 00:50:36.033
There is a fifth meeting of all
the Hubs, so this would be all

00:50:36.033 --> 00:50:40.037
the funded folks will be getting
together from November 3rd

00:50:40.037 --> 00:50:43.540
to 5th in D.C.

00:50:43.540 --> 00:50:46.610
Right after that, another
initiative that actually also

00:50:46.610 --> 00:50:49.914
comes out of the White House is
trying to bring... again, this

00:50:49.914 --> 00:50:54.752
is all in the vein of trying to
not only connect data scientists

00:50:54.752 --> 00:50:59.623
with data, but also trying to
get more data out so that people

00:50:59.623 --> 00:51:03.193
have access to these assets that
they can do their research.

00:51:03.193 --> 00:51:06.230
One way we want to do
this is through a meetup

00:51:06.230 --> 00:51:07.064
of meetup groups.

00:51:07.064 --> 00:51:09.700
So, there are many data
science meetup groups

00:51:09.700 --> 00:51:10.968
around the country.

00:51:10.968 --> 00:51:13.404
There's a data science Meetup
group in San Francisco that has

00:51:13.404 --> 00:51:15.372
a membership of 8,000.

00:51:15.372 --> 00:51:21.011
There's one in Boston, there's
New York City, Atlanta, and the

00:51:21.011 --> 00:51:23.280
smallest is 2,000-3,000 members.

00:51:23.280 --> 00:51:26.784
So, if you put all of these
folks together you're talking

00:51:26.784 --> 00:51:29.787
about something like
30,000-40,000 membership of

00:51:29.787 --> 00:51:32.523
people who are saying they're
interested in data science.

00:51:32.523 --> 00:51:36.260
A lot of these people are folks
who work in industry and they

00:51:36.260 --> 00:51:39.563
want to just do data science
problems as something

00:51:39.563 --> 00:51:42.099
that's of interest.

00:51:42.099 --> 00:51:44.935
So, we are bringing together the
coordinators of all of these

00:51:44.935 --> 00:51:48.439
meetup groups together in D.C.
and that's going to be right

00:51:48.439 --> 00:51:51.375
after the Hubs meeting, so we're
trying to connect the Hubs folks

00:51:51.375 --> 00:51:55.746
to the Meetup group folks so the
Hubs get to meet all the Meetup

00:51:55.746 --> 00:51:58.716
groups that are in their regions
and that's another way to try

00:51:58.716 --> 00:52:01.118
and amplify the message.

00:52:01.118 --> 00:52:03.420
The Big Data program,
itself, will have a PI

00:52:03.420 --> 00:52:05.522
meeting in February.

00:52:06.824 --> 00:52:12.496
A big area that is already there
but is definitely looming in the

00:52:12.496 --> 00:52:16.100
future, is Internet of Things,
so you expect most of the data

00:52:16.100 --> 00:52:18.769
that's going to be coming in the
future is going to be coming

00:52:18.769 --> 00:52:22.406
from all of these devices,
whether they're in factories or

00:52:22.406 --> 00:52:26.477
in your house or on your body or
in your car, etcetera, right?

00:52:26.477 --> 00:52:31.582
So, we have started a series of
workshops just focused on the

00:52:31.582 --> 00:52:33.517
data problem with
the Internet of Things.

00:52:33.517 --> 00:52:35.319
There are a lot
of issues with IoT.

00:52:35.319 --> 00:52:38.522
If you just do a search on
IoT you'll see, I don't know,

00:52:38.522 --> 00:52:41.692
maybe hundreds of
meetings and conferences.

00:52:41.692 --> 00:52:44.895
A lot of the issues are at the
device level, at the networking

00:52:44.895 --> 00:52:47.331
level, security,
privacy, etcetera.

00:52:47.331 --> 00:52:52.970
We are interested on just - it's
almost like the theory of data

00:52:52.970 --> 00:52:54.838
in the Internet of Things.

00:52:54.838 --> 00:52:57.708
One of the issues, for example,
is the latency of data.

00:52:57.708 --> 00:53:00.811
So, if you have an Internet of
Things, data is coming at you at

00:53:00.811 --> 00:53:02.679
different rates
for various reasons.

00:53:02.679 --> 00:53:04.581
The network latencies are there.

00:53:04.581 --> 00:53:07.618
Part of the network may be down.

00:53:07.618 --> 00:53:08.819
You might be getting good data.

00:53:08.819 --> 00:53:10.587
You might be getting bad data.

00:53:10.587 --> 00:53:13.257
But whatever applications are
running on top of that, they

00:53:13.257 --> 00:53:18.295
have to make decisions and they
have to do things within some

00:53:18.295 --> 00:53:22.132
time frame, so there's the issue
of how do you build those kind

00:53:22.132 --> 00:53:25.035
of systems and make those
decisions in the presence of all

00:53:25.035 --> 00:53:28.906
sorts of latency issues
and data issues?

00:53:28.906 --> 00:53:30.240
So, we're trying
to focus on that.

00:53:30.240 --> 00:53:34.311
We had one meeting on the
Industrial Internet, and that

00:53:34.311 --> 00:53:40.584
was hosted by Cisco in San Jose,
and we are planning to have

00:53:40.584 --> 00:53:42.419
a couple more.

00:53:42.419 --> 00:53:45.522
One on Smart and Connected
Communities, and we are thinking

00:53:45.522 --> 00:53:48.592
Chicago might be the place and
we've actually talked to the

00:53:48.592 --> 00:53:52.796
folks who do the Internet array
of things, Charlie Catlett,

00:53:52.796 --> 00:53:56.300
and then, of course,
personalized medicine.

00:53:57.935 --> 00:53:59.470
Data Science and Education.

00:53:59.470 --> 00:54:01.171
I said I was going to say
something about that.

00:54:01.171 --> 00:54:04.408
So, that's a big, big deal and I
don't know if this is happening

00:54:04.408 --> 00:54:09.313
at Yale, but many campuses, some
of them already have rolled out

00:54:09.313 --> 00:54:13.117
data science programs, and
many are talking about doing

00:54:13.117 --> 00:54:15.352
data science programs, right?

00:54:15.352 --> 00:54:19.289
So, we had one data science
education workshop recently at

00:54:19.289 --> 00:54:24.795
the University of Washington,
which was really exciting.

00:54:24.795 --> 00:54:28.165
The model of that workshop was,
we funded, there was about

00:54:28.165 --> 00:54:31.502
100 different graduate students
from different disciplines.

00:54:31.502 --> 00:54:35.005
In fact, it was everything from
humanities and one of them was

00:54:35.005 --> 00:54:40.744
an artist who does data based
art, you know, an outside art,

00:54:40.744 --> 00:54:45.415
sculptures and things like this,
all the way to your usual

00:54:45.415 --> 00:54:46.817
physical science type folks.

00:54:46.817 --> 00:54:48.485
The whole range.

00:54:49.186 --> 00:54:51.922
And it was really interesting
to see the dynamics

00:54:51.922 --> 00:54:52.523
among the students.

00:54:52.523 --> 00:54:56.126
They were just grad students
coming together and all

00:54:56.126 --> 00:54:59.196
realizing that, you know,
they're all doing stuff for data

00:54:59.196 --> 00:55:02.266
and many of them might be using
techniques that others could be

00:55:02.266 --> 00:55:04.968
- you know, that you could learn
from what the others are doing.

00:55:04.968 --> 00:55:08.872
So, it's a little bit like
what sort of happens here,

00:55:08.872 --> 00:55:11.642
except you have 100 grad
students from all over the

00:55:11.642 --> 00:55:12.843
country from different schools.

00:55:12.843 --> 00:55:16.079
So, that is a very interesting
activity and I think we'll

00:55:16.079 --> 00:55:20.484
continue that as
an annual activity.

00:55:20.484 --> 00:55:24.388
Another workshop that I'm
planning, and we have multiple

00:55:24.388 --> 00:55:27.758
folks at NSF involved...
the education directorate,

00:55:27.758 --> 00:55:31.161
the statistics folks, social
science, computer science,

00:55:31.161 --> 00:55:35.566
and also NIH... is what
we are calling Envisioning

00:55:35.566 --> 00:55:36.700
the Data Science Discipline.

00:55:36.700 --> 00:55:43.640
So, if you step back and think,
so what's happening right now on

00:55:43.640 --> 00:55:46.677
a lot of campuses is people
see a market opportunity.

00:55:46.677 --> 00:55:50.047
There's clearly a need to
produce students with a certain

00:55:50.047 --> 00:55:53.650
set of skills, and so, they're
trying to connect the dots.

00:55:53.650 --> 00:55:56.553
They're trying to connect the
dots from, okay, we have these

00:55:56.553 --> 00:55:57.921
kinds of skills on campus.

00:55:57.921 --> 00:55:59.923
We have a computer science
department, we have stats,

00:55:59.923 --> 00:56:02.259
we have management, and we
want to produce, you know,

00:56:02.259 --> 00:56:04.761
these kind of students,
so how do I make progress?

00:56:04.761 --> 00:56:05.929
Which is fine.

00:56:05.929 --> 00:56:10.968
It's a very pragmatic way to put
together a data science program.

00:56:10.968 --> 00:56:13.437
I think we are trying to
say something different with

00:56:13.437 --> 00:56:14.771
this Envisioning workshop.

00:56:14.771 --> 00:56:18.575
We are trying to say, okay, if
data science was a discipline,

00:56:18.575 --> 00:56:23.013
what would it actually look
like, and what would be the

00:56:23.013 --> 00:56:25.649
fundamentals of data science
that make it different than

00:56:25.649 --> 00:56:28.151
computer science and
statistics, etcetera?

00:56:28.151 --> 00:56:31.488
It's undoubtedly an intersection
of computer science, statistics,

00:56:31.488 --> 00:56:35.626
and social science, but it's
more of a visioning exercise.

00:56:35.626 --> 00:56:40.964
And from that, other things
may flow, other workshops

00:56:40.964 --> 00:56:42.332
may happen, etcetera.

00:56:42.332 --> 00:56:45.936
And we requested the
National Academy of Science

00:56:45.936 --> 00:56:48.305
to run this workshop,
so that's coming up.

00:56:48.305 --> 00:56:52.943
If you have thoughts or
interests let me know.

00:56:52.943 --> 00:56:56.313
We already have a list
of folks that we'd like to

00:56:56.313 --> 00:56:57.981
invite to this.

00:56:57.981 --> 00:57:01.218
And as you may or may not, that
every directorate at NSF has an

00:57:01.218 --> 00:57:04.187
advisory committee, which is
essentially the folks from the

00:57:04.187 --> 00:57:09.960
community who provide advice to
the directorate at NSF, and CISE

00:57:09.960 --> 00:57:13.630
has an advisory committee and we
just now set up a subcommittee

00:57:13.630 --> 00:57:17.634
of that focused on
data science education.

00:57:17.634 --> 00:57:20.804
And so, again, they are also
looking at, you know, more from

00:57:20.804 --> 00:57:24.508
a computer science point of
view, what should we be doing,

00:57:24.508 --> 00:57:28.278
what should we be teaching, what
are the facilities that need to

00:57:28.278 --> 00:57:32.516
be in place to teach data
science effectively, and so on?

00:57:35.686 --> 00:57:37.254
Last couple of slides.

00:57:37.254 --> 00:57:40.223
So, I did say that there is
interagency activity going on,

00:57:40.223 --> 00:57:43.160
so, one of my jobs at
NSF is to actually co-chair

00:57:43.160 --> 00:57:44.995
this interagency group.

00:57:44.995 --> 00:57:47.964
We just call it the Big Data
Senior Steering Group,

00:57:47.964 --> 00:57:50.701
which was actually set
up as part of the original

00:57:50.701 --> 00:57:53.303
Obama Administration Initiative.

00:57:53.303 --> 00:57:57.007
The group is co-chaired
by NSF and NIH.

00:57:57.007 --> 00:58:00.077
So, it includes over 18
different federal R&amp;D agencies

00:58:00.077 --> 00:58:02.846
who are all interested in
big data, so it's the usual.

00:58:02.846 --> 00:58:07.851
You know, we have NASA,
NOAA, DOD, DOE...

00:58:07.851 --> 00:58:13.090
Department of Energy,
NSA and NIST and so on.

00:58:15.525 --> 00:58:17.961
These are just informational.

00:58:17.961 --> 00:58:27.037
So, that's going on and actually
we have monthly meetings, and we

00:58:27.037 --> 00:58:30.107
actually invite external
speakers to come and speak and

00:58:30.107 --> 00:58:32.976
I think there's an invitation
out to Bob and the NOAA folks to

00:58:32.976 --> 00:58:37.748
come and talk about the NOAA
Cloud that they went through.

00:58:37.748 --> 00:58:41.385
Another activity going on is the
NIST Public Working Group on Big

00:58:41.385 --> 00:58:43.653
Data, which is actually
trying to develop standards,

00:58:43.653 --> 00:58:45.355
and actually I was
a co-chair of that.

00:58:45.355 --> 00:58:48.959
I have to say. I'm a
non-active co-chair right now.

00:58:48.959 --> 00:58:50.961
I think one of the things that
happens in government is once

00:58:50.961 --> 00:58:55.098
your name is there, it's
actually hard to remove it.

00:58:55.098 --> 00:58:58.668
But, it's been interesting to
observe that because the field

00:58:58.668 --> 00:59:02.005
is moving so fast that every
time they get down to defining

00:59:02.005 --> 00:59:07.244
something it's moved along
and keeping up is very hard.

00:59:14.217 --> 00:59:16.253
So, we do collaborate with NIH.

00:59:16.253 --> 00:59:20.056
There is a program that NSF
recently announced with the

00:59:20.056 --> 00:59:23.660
Statistics Institution and NIH
called Quantitative Approaches

00:59:23.660 --> 00:59:24.828
to Biomedical Big Data.

00:59:24.828 --> 00:59:28.031
Is anyone from Yale
participating in that,

00:59:28.031 --> 00:59:28.698
do you know?

00:59:28.698 --> 00:59:29.933
Okay.

00:59:29.933 --> 00:59:34.337
They have funded a number
of planning grants, I believe,

00:59:34.337 --> 00:59:37.774
and this is, again, NIH
interested in connecting with

00:59:37.774 --> 00:59:41.778
the statistics community to see
what kind of statistical methods

00:59:41.778 --> 00:59:43.847
could be used for the
kinds of big data that they

00:59:43.847 --> 00:59:46.116
have in biomedicine.

00:59:46.116 --> 00:59:51.922
And I was mentioning to someone
earlier today, in fact, had a

00:59:51.922 --> 00:59:56.393
meeting with NIH, Phil Bourne,
who was your speaker last year.

00:59:56.393 --> 00:59:59.596
Phil and I have been talking
along with some folks at NSF

00:59:59.596 --> 01:00:03.733
about doing a workshop, and this
one will be in conjunction with

01:00:03.733 --> 01:00:10.207
the UK Research Councils
on how to try and expose more

01:00:10.207 --> 01:00:13.510
biomedical data for the
community to do research on.

01:00:13.510 --> 01:00:18.648
So, the UK folks have, you know,
good datasets, I think because

01:00:18.648 --> 01:00:22.018
of their national health
service and so on.

01:00:22.018 --> 01:00:26.022
Increasingly, NIH is trying
to put out more datasets.

01:00:26.022 --> 01:00:28.725
There's something called
healthdata.gov you might be

01:00:28.725 --> 01:00:32.996
familiar with, where
agencies are trying to

01:00:32.996 --> 01:00:34.931
publish health data.

01:00:34.931 --> 01:00:38.068
And so, it's going to
be an NSF-funded workshop.

01:00:38.068 --> 01:00:42.138
We'll actually run it at NIH and
we'll have participation from

01:00:42.138 --> 01:00:44.641
the UK Research
Councils on that.

01:00:46.877 --> 01:00:51.014
So, my last slide is some
set of things where we

01:00:51.014 --> 01:00:52.382
need your inputs.

01:00:52.382 --> 01:00:54.451
So, this, I think,
is a real challenge.

01:00:54.451 --> 01:00:57.220
How do we get our hands on data?

01:00:57.220 --> 01:00:59.089
So, we need your ideas.

01:00:59.089 --> 01:01:01.391
We need to release more data.

01:01:01.391 --> 01:01:06.897
We need to put it out there and,
as you know, it's not just,

01:01:06.897 --> 01:01:11.001
I mean, the data has to sit next
to computing, right, so it could

01:01:11.001 --> 01:01:13.169
be a hadoop style or whatever
the style or platform is,

01:01:13.169 --> 01:01:16.806
but computing has to be there
with the data, so it's not just

01:01:16.806 --> 01:01:20.544
publishing files on an FTB
site or something like this.

01:01:20.544 --> 01:01:23.246
How do we build
platforms at scale?

01:01:23.246 --> 01:01:26.683
As I said, I've actually started
a conversation with some of the

01:01:26.683 --> 01:01:29.753
folks in the community,
and this is more the computer

01:01:29.753 --> 01:01:35.358
science folks, as to
how would we do this?

01:01:35.358 --> 01:01:39.162
And, especially in an
environment where, and as far as

01:01:39.162 --> 01:01:42.198
I'm concerned the country
doesn't care for R&amp;D, right?

01:01:42.198 --> 01:01:46.503
If you sit at NSF, somebody
said the other day, in fact,

01:01:46.503 --> 01:01:51.708
our assistant director,
said Flat is the new Positive,

01:01:51.708 --> 01:01:52.842
or something like that.

01:01:52.842 --> 01:01:55.178
If you are flat
you will be happy.

01:01:55.178 --> 01:01:59.149
If your budgets are flat
it's supposed to be happy.

01:01:59.149 --> 01:02:01.184
And our budgets are flat.

01:02:01.184 --> 01:02:05.555
And I was at this workshop at
the University of Washington,

01:02:05.555 --> 01:02:08.158
one of the faculty said, you
know, NSF doesn't understand

01:02:08.158 --> 01:02:10.760
that there is
inflation in this world.

01:02:10.760 --> 01:02:13.997
And, by the way, I
was a PI till a year ago.

01:02:13.997 --> 01:02:16.032
I was on the same
side a year ago.

01:02:16.032 --> 01:02:18.969
It's thought that NSF
doesn't understand.

01:02:18.969 --> 01:02:20.337
NSF budgets are flat.

01:02:20.337 --> 01:02:23.006
You know, if you guys keep
getting 2% or 3% raises

01:02:23.006 --> 01:02:23.673
or even higher.

01:02:23.673 --> 01:02:25.675
Private schools probably
give you lots of big raises -

01:02:25.675 --> 01:02:28.712
University of California
doesn't give huge raises.

01:02:29.746 --> 01:02:30.480
Sorry.

01:02:30.480 --> 01:02:34.818
That means, you know, you're
going to put less effort on a

01:02:34.818 --> 01:02:37.220
project because the
amount of money is fixed.

01:02:37.220 --> 01:02:39.322
So, if you want to fix that I
think you need to go and talk to

01:02:39.322 --> 01:02:40.924
people in Washington, D.C.

01:02:40.924 --> 01:02:42.258
So, that's my pitch.

01:02:42.258 --> 01:02:42.926
Okay.

01:02:42.926 --> 01:02:48.398
So, we're in the day of flat
budgets but we want to have

01:02:48.398 --> 01:02:50.767
these new initiatives, you know,
we want to create new big data

01:02:50.767 --> 01:02:54.537
platforms, and I mean, you can
imagine back to the day, 20-30

01:02:54.537 --> 01:02:57.340
years ago, when somebody said,
and this was Sid Karin and

01:02:57.340 --> 01:03:00.143
Larry Smarr, that we
need super computers.

01:03:00.143 --> 01:03:04.347
We need super computer centers
and new money materialized at

01:03:04.347 --> 01:03:07.384
NSF to fund super
computers, okay?

01:03:07.384 --> 01:03:08.785
This is what we need today.

01:03:08.785 --> 01:03:11.688
We need big data platforms
but how do we make it happen,

01:03:11.688 --> 01:03:13.390
so we need your help.

01:03:13.390 --> 01:03:16.459
Another issue, and I think some
of you may be involved with it,

01:03:16.459 --> 01:03:19.696
but I know there are other
research folks very much in this

01:03:19.696 --> 01:03:21.931
area of data ownership.

01:03:21.931 --> 01:03:25.735
You know, who owns the data,
including, in today's world,

01:03:25.735 --> 01:03:28.505
what's happening to
all of us right now.

01:03:28.505 --> 01:03:30.106
Somebody, again, Bob.

01:03:30.106 --> 01:03:32.642
All these references
are going back to Bob.

01:03:32.642 --> 01:03:35.278
I think you mentioned something
about do you know where

01:03:35.278 --> 01:03:36.680
your data is.

01:03:36.680 --> 01:03:43.286
So, there's this issue of how do
we tackle that problem, right?

01:03:43.286 --> 01:03:48.425
It's not just the issue of
keeping data anonymized and

01:03:48.425 --> 01:03:52.796
private and giving protected
access but it's also who's

01:03:52.796 --> 01:03:53.797
the controlling power?

01:03:53.797 --> 01:03:59.135
You know, who's ruling
in this case?

01:03:59.135 --> 01:04:02.739
And the last thing I'll say,
we actually fund a group,

01:04:02.739 --> 01:04:09.145
and it's a multi university,
but I think based out of NYU,

01:04:09.145 --> 01:04:11.247
and other folks.

01:04:11.247 --> 01:04:15.385
There's folks from UC Irvine,
maybe Johns Hopkins.

01:04:15.385 --> 01:04:16.720
I can't remember
all the schools.

01:04:16.720 --> 01:04:18.555
I can't remember if Yale is
there on it, but it's called

01:04:18.555 --> 01:04:20.790
Big Data Ethics and Society.

01:04:20.790 --> 01:04:24.694
So, I talked about
data science education.

01:04:24.694 --> 01:04:27.564
I mean, I really think that
data science has three legs.

01:04:27.564 --> 01:04:30.867
It's computer science,
statistics, and social science,

01:04:30.867 --> 01:04:32.168
especially the ethics part.

01:04:32.168 --> 01:04:37.073
The biggest Achilles heel for
data science is going to be on

01:04:37.073 --> 01:04:41.344
the ethics side because the
first time somebody, not that

01:04:41.344 --> 01:04:44.314
it's already not happened
already, but, you know,

01:04:44.314 --> 01:04:48.485
the first time someone goes and
messes up data it's going to

01:04:48.485 --> 01:04:50.353
be a huge, huge issue.

01:04:50.353 --> 01:04:57.160
So, I think of ethics as a
very big topic and so, again,

01:04:57.160 --> 01:05:00.463
whatever ideas you have,
the more you can teach it,

01:05:00.463 --> 01:05:02.532
the more you can
do in it it's great.

01:05:02.532 --> 01:05:05.735
As I said, we fund this group
called Big Data Ethics and

01:05:05.735 --> 01:05:10.473
Society and we've asked them
to come and present at the

01:05:10.473 --> 01:05:12.542
Big Data PI's meeting.

01:05:12.542 --> 01:05:15.378
We may also ask them to attend
the Hubs meeting so that they

01:05:15.378 --> 01:05:18.748
can sort of - it's a very good
group of folks and hopefully

01:05:18.748 --> 01:05:19.916
they can spread this message.

01:05:19.916 --> 01:05:22.519
So, if you have folks on this
campus interested in the ethics

01:05:22.519 --> 01:05:26.956
issue I think they should
really get connected to this.

01:05:26.956 --> 01:05:29.325
So, I apologize for
such a dry talk.

01:05:29.325 --> 01:05:31.795
I didn't realize it was going
to be this dry, but it's very

01:05:31.795 --> 01:05:32.595
interesting to me.

01:05:32.595 --> 01:05:33.429
So.

01:05:34.597 --> 01:05:39.569
(Applause)

01:05:54.350 --> 01:05:56.352
So, I also did not
find that dry at all.

01:05:56.352 --> 01:05:57.387
I thought it very interesting.

01:05:57.387 --> 01:05:59.322
Thank you.

01:05:59.322 --> 01:06:03.860
So, Yale is actually a part of
the northeast regional big data

01:06:03.860 --> 01:06:09.799
hub along with Columbia, and we
currently have three faculty

01:06:09.799 --> 01:06:12.602
that are helping
drive that initiative.

01:06:12.602 --> 01:06:16.272
Can you speak a little bit about
the skill sets for the other

01:06:16.272 --> 01:06:18.775
folks that will be needed to
help support and propel that

01:06:18.775 --> 01:06:21.277
initiative should the
funding be received?

01:06:21.277 --> 01:06:22.212
Okay.

01:06:24.981 --> 01:06:27.951
So, that's a very good question.

01:06:27.951 --> 01:06:33.489
I think I had, at the bottom of
that slide, something about the

01:06:33.489 --> 01:06:37.393
Hugs are there to enable
you to do something that

01:06:37.393 --> 01:06:41.898
you couldn't otherwise
do by yourself, that is.

01:06:41.898 --> 01:06:45.235
And when I say you,
it's even the campus.

01:06:45.235 --> 01:06:52.342
So, it's really regional in that
sense, so I'm not sure what all

01:06:52.342 --> 01:06:54.978
the issues here are, but I know
about water in San Diego,

01:06:54.978 --> 01:06:57.313
so let me pick water in
California as an issue.

01:06:57.313 --> 01:06:59.782
So, if you want to try and
address the water issue in

01:06:59.782 --> 01:07:06.556
California, you need
partnerships across the state,

01:07:06.556 --> 01:07:08.658
both in academia
and also agencies.

01:07:08.658 --> 01:07:13.796
So, I think the kind of folks
you need to be involved,

01:07:13.796 --> 01:07:18.368
the Hubs program is very
different than what NSF

01:07:18.368 --> 01:07:19.669
has done before.

01:07:19.669 --> 01:07:23.273
It's almost like an altruistic -
well, it's an organizational

01:07:23.273 --> 01:07:26.476
thing, but it's also altruistic,
so PI's are not very good at

01:07:26.476 --> 01:07:27.577
being altruistic, right?

01:07:27.577 --> 01:07:31.381
I mean, I've been a PI, so PI's
are all about, okay what's in

01:07:31.381 --> 01:07:33.549
the budget for me?

01:07:33.549 --> 01:07:38.154
But here, we are talking about
how do you bring together all

01:07:38.154 --> 01:07:42.892
sorts of different actors to
create a more complete picture

01:07:42.892 --> 01:07:45.328
of whatever it is that
you're trying to solve.

01:07:45.328 --> 01:07:50.733
So, if you can get faculty
who are interested in that.

01:07:52.268 --> 01:07:55.371
Well, one of the things
I find, especially in the

01:07:55.371 --> 01:07:59.142
interdisciplinary work,
including I've met some recent

01:07:59.142 --> 01:08:02.745
young faculty at UCSD, is that
the young faculty have a lot of

01:08:02.745 --> 01:08:06.950
energy in this interdisciplinary
stuff, but I'm not sure if Hubs

01:08:06.950 --> 01:08:10.053
is where you'd want
to engage them.

01:08:11.454 --> 01:08:15.658
If you can find that senior
faculty, maybe Bob, but Bob is

01:08:15.658 --> 01:08:19.762
not so senior, but, anyway,
if you can find the experienced

01:08:19.762 --> 01:08:25.068
faculty who is also interested
in doing something that's

01:08:25.068 --> 01:08:27.737
broader than just their own
program, those would be the

01:08:27.737 --> 01:08:30.073
right kind of people.

01:08:30.073 --> 01:08:34.911
And obviously, that means they
have to have an interest that's,

01:08:34.911 --> 01:08:39.215
you know, we're using -
and you'll see it in the

01:08:39.215 --> 01:08:43.319
solicitation as well, we use the
term National Priority Areas,

01:08:43.319 --> 01:08:47.657
so the White House OSTP has
announced a number of priority

01:08:47.657 --> 01:08:51.027
areas... precision medicine
is one, global climate change

01:08:51.027 --> 01:08:54.497
is one, smart manufacturing,
smart interconnected

01:08:54.497 --> 01:08:56.099
communities... these
are all considered to

01:08:56.099 --> 01:08:57.133
be priority areas.

01:08:57.133 --> 01:09:00.770
So, if you have folks who are
interested in working in those

01:09:00.770 --> 01:09:05.842
priority areas, but not so
narrowly interested in just

01:09:05.842 --> 01:09:09.045
their own lab but actually
trying to reach out and help,

01:09:09.045 --> 01:09:12.281
and I'm sure there
are many here.

01:09:12.281 --> 01:09:14.951
I certainly know folks at
other places who are like that.

01:09:14.951 --> 01:09:18.221
Those would be the
best folks to engage.

01:09:23.092 --> 01:09:27.530
Is this model being extended
to other parts of the world?

01:09:27.530 --> 01:09:28.197
Good question.

01:09:28.197 --> 01:09:32.335
So, actually, in the Spokes
solicitation, which we are right

01:09:32.335 --> 01:09:34.771
now working on putting it
together, we received interest

01:09:34.771 --> 01:09:38.908
from international groups who
want to participate in this.

01:09:38.908 --> 01:09:43.513
So, the Hubs didn't say, but the
Spokes will actually say that we

01:09:43.513 --> 01:09:46.616
encourage international
partnerships, and so,

01:09:46.616 --> 01:09:48.017
there could be some topics.

01:09:48.017 --> 01:09:52.221
Actually, smart cities is a good
one, right, because there's a

01:09:52.221 --> 01:09:59.796
faculty member at MIT
who visited Singapore.

01:09:59.796 --> 01:10:03.566
Singapore has a vague smart
city initiative and what they

01:10:03.566 --> 01:10:08.037
realized is because of the way
everything gets controlled in

01:10:08.037 --> 01:10:12.575
Singapore, I suppose, they can
control how data is released.

01:10:12.575 --> 01:10:15.111
And they realized that the data
that they have is actually an

01:10:15.111 --> 01:10:18.981
asset for international
collaborations because not many

01:10:18.981 --> 01:10:22.418
cities or states have
that kind of data that they

01:10:22.418 --> 01:10:24.921
have access to.

01:10:24.921 --> 01:10:27.857
So, for example, in Smart and
Connected Cities, we have a

01:10:27.857 --> 01:10:30.393
connection with Singapore
and we can get all their data

01:10:30.393 --> 01:10:32.161
and we can do some
very fancy, you know,

01:10:32.161 --> 01:10:34.764
parking optimization
or whatever.

01:10:34.764 --> 01:10:35.765
That would be interesting.

01:10:35.765 --> 01:10:40.770
So, I think the
Spokes will ask for

01:10:40.770 --> 01:10:42.905
encourage international
partnerships.

01:10:42.905 --> 01:10:45.975
We are also finding a lot of
interest from other agencies.

01:10:45.975 --> 01:10:49.312
I'm not sure of the solicitation
with the state or not, but some

01:10:49.312 --> 01:10:52.949
of the agencies are even saying
that they may do their own

01:10:52.949 --> 01:10:56.285
solicitation which, in some
ways, is actually simpler,

01:10:56.285 --> 01:10:57.787
but then connect to the Hubs.

01:10:57.787 --> 01:11:02.291
So, you know, DHS is also very
interested in Smart City issues,

01:11:02.291 --> 01:11:04.894
and actually, I mentioned the
Internet of Things workshop,

01:11:04.894 --> 01:11:08.297
I don't think I mentioned the
next series of workshops we are

01:11:08.297 --> 01:11:11.567
doing is with NHS, so
it'll be NSF-DHS jointly

01:11:11.567 --> 01:11:13.603
doing IoT workshops.

01:11:13.603 --> 01:11:17.106
I won't be surprised if someone
like that will put something out

01:11:17.106 --> 01:11:20.710
and say, you know, we have an
opportunity here and we would

01:11:20.710 --> 01:11:23.446
like to connect this
to the NSF Hubs.

01:11:30.219 --> 01:11:31.621
Thank you for your talk.

01:11:31.621 --> 01:11:34.557
Something you said I thought was
interesting, or it was on your

01:11:34.557 --> 01:11:37.827
slide, is that you said data is
a new instrument, and I'm coming

01:11:37.827 --> 01:11:40.663
from a bioinformatics
background and when I think of

01:11:40.663 --> 01:11:43.166
bioinformatics compared
to traditional life sciences

01:11:43.166 --> 01:11:46.469
I think of instead of
studying living things,

01:11:46.469 --> 01:11:48.371
I'm studying the data.

01:11:48.371 --> 01:11:52.642
I'm serving the data and the
metadata on the data, but I

01:11:52.642 --> 01:11:55.411
usually think of the instruments
that I'm using as mathematics,

01:11:55.411 --> 01:11:56.712
statistics, and
computer science.

01:11:56.712 --> 01:11:58.881
So, I was wondering if you could
talk a little bit about how you

01:11:58.881 --> 01:12:01.617
see data as the actual
instrument instead of the

01:12:01.617 --> 01:12:04.487
thing being measured.

01:12:04.487 --> 01:12:04.921
Right.

01:12:04.921 --> 01:12:06.455
And, you know...

01:12:09.525 --> 01:12:16.699
I think what I really mean
there is, there's a lot of

01:12:16.699 --> 01:12:19.035
opportunity, and I think in
astronomy this has already being

01:12:19.035 --> 01:12:22.672
done, where you can
discover new things just by

01:12:22.672 --> 01:12:24.340
analyzing the data.

01:12:24.974 --> 01:12:28.010
So, you don't have to go
to the instrument like a

01:12:28.010 --> 01:12:29.745
telescope or something.

01:12:29.745 --> 01:12:35.051
Of course, somebody went and
brought it together, the data

01:12:35.051 --> 01:12:38.588
could just be click streams and
maybe you observe some social

01:12:38.588 --> 01:12:41.757
phenomena by observing
something in the click stream.

01:12:41.757 --> 01:12:42.959
That's kind of what I mean.

01:12:42.959 --> 01:12:47.697
So, it's an instrument to study
a phenomena in that sense.

01:12:49.832 --> 01:12:51.734
Can you join me in
thanking our speaker?

01:12:51.734 --> 01:12:55.404
